WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.840
- So I wanna start everything
with just a one-minute clip

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.798
written by our special guest,
from the HBO show Treme,

00:00:18.798 --> 00:00:21.233
so let's do that, cool.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.313
Hopefully the volume will work, okay.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:28.870
- But we're gonna have potholes

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:32.549
that just swallow your whole
fuckin' car, you feel me?

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:33.413
- You're obsessive about potholes.

00:00:33.413 --> 00:00:36.737
- Somebody's gotta call
these motherfuckers out.

00:00:36.737 --> 00:00:37.707
- I agree.

00:00:37.707 --> 00:00:38.783
- [Presenter] He's
running for city council.

00:00:38.783 --> 00:00:40.203
- You agree, really?

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.490
Jacques Morial, the scion
of a great political dynasty

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:45.323
agrees with me.

00:00:45.323 --> 00:00:48.960
- Hey, that shit is funny,
Greased Palm Sunday.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.210
- Pot for potholes, don't forget that one.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.390
- The satire, it's good,
but you have a chance

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:54.805
to raise some serious questions

00:00:54.805 --> 00:00:58.210
that none of these other
motherfuckers are gonna touch.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:59.950
- I do?
- Look,

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.110
here we got some serious
challenges, we've got

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:02.943
(garbled audio)

00:01:02.943 --> 00:01:05.300
we got expropriation of people's homes,

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.568
we got when they're gonna
reopen Charity Hospital,

00:01:07.568 --> 00:01:09.149
and then there's this big nasty question

00:01:09.149 --> 00:01:10.352
about who can come home and who can't

00:01:10.352 --> 00:01:11.247
come home.
- Right.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:16.610
Here's an idea; why don't
you be my campaign manager?

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:17.607
- Not gonna happen.

00:01:19.094 --> 00:01:21.073
But look, what I can
do is I can hook you up

00:01:21.073 --> 00:01:23.840
with some real effective public exposure

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.443
so we get these issues on the table.

00:01:26.443 --> 00:01:29.700
Now, that's if you really
wanna kick it up a notch.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.900
- Hell yeah.
- You should go for it, Davis.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.556
- Do you really think I can win this?

00:01:37.556 --> 00:01:39.510
- The election?

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:41.123
No fucking way.

00:01:41.123 --> 00:01:42.623
- [Presenter] (laughing) Okay.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.300
- I was just checking.
- Yeah, that's it.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.133
Alright, great.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:57.280
So that scene, written by
our guest author Tom Piazza,

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.960
gives you just I think, a, thank you,

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.040
gives you just the smallest
sense of his talent,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.720
his ability to capture human ambition,

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.350
making something out of nothing,

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.660
small but stinging disappointment,

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.630
and the familiar strangeness
of racial politics

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:15.820
in the broadest sense of the term.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:19.440
It's also really funny, and appropriately

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.360
delightfully drops the F-bomb.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.433
Okay, so I'll rewind now a little bit,

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.910
thank you all so much for being here,

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:31.260
I have a more formal
version of that thank you,

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.120
so let me read it.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.150
The Franke Seminars and
Lectures are intended

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:38.010
to present important
topics in the humanities

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.220
to a wide and general audience, and to tie

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.470
interdisciplinary undergraduate education

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.980
to the work of distinguished
visiting scholars.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.960
Endowed by Richard and
Barbara Franke in 2004,

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.700
this series is organized each year

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.860
in conjunction with an
undergraduate seminar

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:55.300
taught by a Yale faculty
member, or Yale faculty members,

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:58.300
and develops a theme of broad
interests in the community.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.290
These seminars form an important component

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.460
of the humanities curriculum,
and we are grateful

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:03.730
for the extraordinary support

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:06.240
that the Frankes continue to
provide for these occasions,

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.440
so thank you to them and
thank you to all of you

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.093
for being here.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:15.560
Mr. Piazza is the author
of an astonishing 12 books,

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.240
including the novels A
Free State, My Cold War,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.840
and City of Refuge, along
with Devil Sent the Rain,

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.690
Music and Writing in Desperate Times,

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:25.200
Why New Orleans Matters,

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.333
and Blues and Trouble; 12 Short Stories.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.840
City of Refuge won the Willy Morris Award

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.060
for Southern fiction,

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.470
and his My Cold War won
the Faulkner Society Award.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:37.570
Why New Orleans Matters received

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:39.370
the Louisiana Endowment for the Humanities

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:40.430
Book of the Year Award,

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:43.360
and Blues and Trouble won
the James Michener Award.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.403
Piazza is a richly
deservedly-decorated author,

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.770
add to this a Grammy.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.220
Mr. Piazza received a
Grammy for his album notes

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:56.660
to Martin Scorsese Presents
the Blues; A Musical Journey.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:59.500
Indeed, Mr. Piazza has been
a longtime preeminent writer

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:02.410
of American music, notably jazz, blues,

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:04.030
bluegrass, and country.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:06.350
These writings as freestanding books

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.690
as articles in The New
York Times, The Atlantic,

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:13.110
and everywhere else, or
part of larger anthologies,

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:15.850
have earned him a whole
other chest of awards,

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:17.240
and of course he's a principal writer

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:18.490
for the HBO series Treme.

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:22.360
Mr. Piazza went to Williams College,

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.500
received an MFA in fiction
from the world-renowned

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.070
Iowa Writer's Workshop,

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.330
and he has been a Writer in Residence

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.380
at several colleges and universities,

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.590
including Hobart and
Williams Smith Colleges

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:33.193
and Loyola University.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.050
For the seminar that I
teach with Crystal Feimster,

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.150
New Orleans and the American Imaginary,

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:42.090
we've read Piazza's City of
Refuge, Why New Orleans Matters,

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:43.690
and his short story Brownsville,

00:04:44.839 --> 00:04:47.250
and we had a really wonderful
conversation with him

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:50.930
before you all got here, so
you're late, but it was great.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:55.030
So here's the tiniest
sliver of what memorizes,

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:56.883
mesmerizes and moved us;

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:00.690
Piazza conjures orbs of feeling in words

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.130
that captivate completely,
even or especially

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:04.890
as they unsettle us.

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.430
His words, his characters, his narratives,

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:11.880
stun in their exposure of
anguish, rage, disorientation,

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.210
and those other emotions
so hard to sit with

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:15.833
but so necessary to read with.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.700
In City of Refuge, drawing
on metaphors of the body

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.090
and humans' in-sor-ia, Piazza's
New Orleans is our friend,

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:26.210
our lover, we are nearly the city's widow,

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.040
and we come to love, long
for, and root for the city

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.420
as we do the two
families, black and white,

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:34.700
who improvised their way to
livable lives after the storm

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:37.970
and the grotesque failures
of government that followed

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:38.943
at every level.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:41.990
And while some of the connective tissue

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:44.990
across our class's Piazza's readings

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:48.450
assembles loss, suffering, and
the resuscitating of selves

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:51.420
and communities alongside and
against atmospheric despair,

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.620
anxiety, and disposability,
I want to also say

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.110
Piazza can be really, really funny.

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:00.360
I found myself snorting in loud,

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:04.340
socially unacceptable
laughter at Jen's raunchy,

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:07.110
beyond raunchy one-liners
in City of Refuge,

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:09.723
and at Aunt Mimi's and
Desiree's reposts in Treme.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:14.390
I'll hand over the mic
with words from our author.

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:17.810
In Why New Orleans Matters
Piazza responds to the question

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:22.510
so often asked in fall 2005,
usually posed rhetorically

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:25.103
with but the thinnest
veil to clock its racism,

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:29.210
why should we rebuild New Orleans?

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:32.140
His answer is the right
one, not only for the city,

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:34.980
but also for all cities,
for infrastructures,

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:37.563
for knowledge production,
for human invention.

00:06:39.397 --> 00:06:42.417
"My answer," he offers,
"began with a notion

00:06:42.417 --> 00:06:45.517
"that no city we know of would
exist in its current form

00:06:45.517 --> 00:06:48.927
"or any other form if it
weren't for human efforts.

00:06:48.927 --> 00:06:51.707
"Everything worthwhile
that humankind has achieved

00:06:51.707 --> 00:06:54.823
"involves a rearguard
action against entropy.

00:06:55.737 --> 00:06:58.237
"Without continual effort
it would all quickly revert

00:06:58.237 --> 00:07:00.137
"to weeds and waste.

00:07:00.137 --> 00:07:04.367
"Anyone who owns a house, tends
a garden, raises children,

00:07:04.367 --> 00:07:07.017
"shaves, or does just about anything else

00:07:07.017 --> 00:07:10.037
"knows this to be true; we
are always, in this sense,

00:07:10.037 --> 00:07:12.260
"pushing back against nature."

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:14.328
Please welcome Tom Piazza.

00:07:14.328 --> 00:07:17.495
(audience applauding)

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:23.740
- Well thank you.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:26.683
Joe, do you want your phone?
- Ah, yeah.

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:30.720
- If I speak in a normal voice like this

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:31.880
you all can hear me?

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:32.793
Yeah, good.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:37.020
Thank you, thank you
very much for coming out,

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:41.900
and I'm so happy to make
so many new acquaintances

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.483
and to see some old friends,
old and dear friends,

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:48.870
what a lovely occasion this is, thank you.

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:53.590
And I wanna thank, oh excuse
me, I wanna especially thank

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:59.560
Joe Fischel and Crystal Feimster
for making this all happen

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.830
in such a graceful and gracious way,

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:07.150
and Micah Cater was indispensable
and incredibly helpful,

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:09.970
and so thank you so much to those three,

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:13.120
and also to the generosity of
Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.890
and thanks also to the
Whitney Humanities Center

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:18.293
for hosting this.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:21.960
So the title of this talk,

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:23.610
I guess you've seen the title of the talk

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:24.830
when it was advertised,

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:29.633
is Improvising Identity; New
Orleans and the American Dream.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:33.810
And I have to apologize in advance

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.770
for delivering a lecture on improvisation

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:40.470
from a prepared written
text, sorry about that,

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:42.920
but anyway, we'll leave
time for discussion

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:44.020
at the end, hopefully.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:48.760
So let me say that I deliver this lecture

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:50.963
with a degree of trepidation.

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:57.390
I'm a novelist, not a cultural
historian or ethnographer,

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:02.390
or not a polemicist certainly,
or even really an essayist.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:07.360
As a novelist I work in an
arena in which opposites

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:10.673
are almost always simultaneously true,

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:15.700
and in which the truth, such
as it is, can't be formulated

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:18.630
in discursive terms, so I always feel

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:22.030
a degree of discomfort with discourse

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:23.850
that is aiming toward a certain point

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:26.940
because I always almost, as a,

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:29.773
I tack in the opposite
direction almost immediately.

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:36.240
I wanna say that the United
States, like New Orleans,

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:38.600
contains staggering and often seemingly

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:41.360
indigestible contradictions.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:43.400
As soon as you say one
thing about the nature

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:47.240
of New Orleans culture,
much less American culture,

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:50.083
it seems that the
opposite is true as well.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:54.700
I would say that both
cultures not only contain,

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:57.590
but are defined by the fact

00:09:57.590 --> 00:09:59.520
of their massive internal contradictions,

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.950
which fact is almost
impossible to engage head-on

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:07.210
without getting overrun by
an armada of exceptions,

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:10.910
qualifications,
counterexamples, equivocations,

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:14.670
mitigations, heavy theoretical
artillery and sniper fire,

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:16.900
outright evidence to the contrary,

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:20.470
briefs for both defense and prosecution.

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:23.770
Instead, in other words
I'm trying to offer

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:26.500
a sustained argument
leading to a grand point,

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:30.210
what I will offer instead
is a series of patrols into,

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:34.100
or raids upon, a territory of questions

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.610
with no clear answers,
at the heart of which

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.010
lies a paradox that has vexed me

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:44.100
for my entire working life
as a writer and as a citizen.

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:48.230
How is it possible for
a nation as diverse,

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:51.700
as maddeningly contradictory
as the United States,

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:56.700
to consider itself a single
entity, a nation with a name.

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:59.850
How can its kaleidoscopically
diverse elements

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:04.320
be recognizably American
each in themselves,

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:08.160
and yet lend themselves
to a term, American,

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:10.830
that is definable only
in terms of the tension

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:12.503
among these elements?

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:18.540
Even the nation's motto, E
Plurbis Unum, from many one,

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:20.580
can be read in opposite ways

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:23.660
like one of those
ambiguous optical illusions

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:24.970
by putting the weight of meaning

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:28.320
either on the many or on the one,

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:30.500
and neither of those readings
is really wholly true,

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:33.650
only the tension between the two readings.

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:36.350
Stop me if I'm getting too
abstract for you too early.

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:39.750
So look, so the point is that these raids

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:43.360
that I'm gonna conduct are
plainly gonna be conducted

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:47.470
over swampy ground, and
sometimes covering the same tract

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:49.350
from different angles.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:52.670
I've found hints of a way of
understanding this paradox

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:55.293
in New Orleans, where
I've lived for 24 years,

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.230
swampy ground, if ever
there were swampy ground.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:03.170
If this 40-minute guerrilla
war that I'm going to

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:06.870
engage in for you here
has anything like a theme

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:08.830
it might be that New
Orleans should be seen

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:12.320
as a representative part
of the American tradition

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:16.543
of self-reinvention,
transformation, and possibility.

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:19.480
But I'm getting ahead
of myself, let me begin

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:22.963
on the more or less solid
ground of my own experience.

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:27.930
New Orleans is a place that
I loved in my imagination

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:30.630
from a very young age.

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:34.070
When I was 11 years old on
Long Island's South Shore,

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:36.450
any South Shore people here?

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:38.430
No Long Island people here?

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:40.119
Oh hell, okay that's it.

00:12:40.119 --> 00:12:41.170
(audience laughing)

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:42.950
No, no, I'll continue, I'll continue.

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:45.520
So alright, on the South
Shore of Long Island,

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:49.133
it's sort of echt-suburbia,
Burger Kings and gas stations,

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:52.630
where I was raised, my
mother bought me a copy

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:57.160
of a book called Jazzmen,
originally published in 1939

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:01.130
and reissued in a bright
orange Harvest Books edition.

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:03.660
I'd been interested in musical performance

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:06.870
since The Beatles came
to the U.S. in early 1964

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:10.130
when I was eight, and like many others

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:13.630
I followed a trail of
recordings and crumbs

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:16.290
from The Beatles, to Chuck
Barry, to Muddy Waters,

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:19.420
to Robert Johnson, and
I think my mom figured

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:22.360
it was a short hop from there to jazz.

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:27.360
In any case, Jazzmen, that
book, is a romantic depiction

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:32.003
of jazz's early years, earliest years,

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:36.780
a collection of hagiographic
essays on early jazz musicians

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:39.190
such as King Oliver, Bix Beiderbecke,

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:41.800
and Louis Armstrong, their working lives,

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:42.940
and recordings and travels.

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:46.780
It contains a fantastic essay
on collecting old 78 records

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:49.750
in dusty antique stores
in dirt road towns,

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:53.270
and most important to me, a
thick section of photographs

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:54.223
in the middle.

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:57.580
The grainy, black and white images

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:02.490
taken back in the 1910s
and 1920s were mesmerizing.

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:05.600
Bands led by men with
names like Buddy Bolden,

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:10.590
and Bunk Johnson, and
Kid Ory, dressed in suits

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:13.110
or band uniforms holding
trumpets, and clarinets,

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:15.000
and trombones, and banjos.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:17.520
These musicians were black and white,

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:20.230
although only rarely did
white and black musicians

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:22.033
appear in the same photographs.

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:26.600
Regardless of skin color,
they gazed into the camera,

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:30.610
through the camera,
straight into posterity,

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:34.430
clearly sure of their own
worth and place in the world.

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:35.840
There were photos of buildings too,

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:38.150
archaic-looking dance halls, and saloons,

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:41.750
and the occasional brothel
with names like Louth Gins,

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:46.370
and Tom Andersons, and
Big 25, and Mahogany Hall,

00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:48.840
streets full of light and shadow

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:51.700
with names that were
in themselves a poetry;

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:55.610
Rampart, Perdido, Burg, well Burgundy,

00:14:55.610 --> 00:14:58.080
which we pronounce as Ber-gone-dy,

00:14:58.080 --> 00:15:00.610
some even named for the Greek muses;

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:04.770
Thalia, Euterpe, Terpsichore, Calliope,

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:07.713
which in New Orleans you
pronounce Cal-ee-ope, by the way.

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:12.020
My grandparents on
either side, both sides,

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:14.580
had come to America in the late 19-teens,

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:17.530
through Ellis Island, eyes firmly locked

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:20.230
on the American Dream,
and yet never quite able

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:23.220
to let go of the umbilicus connecting them

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:25.990
to the literal and figurative Old Country.

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:28.030
They presided over long Sunday dinners

00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:31.230
with aunts and uncles,
recycling ancient resentments

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:34.980
and gossip, and had endless conversations

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:38.150
about this or that other cousin,
or uncle, or neighborhood,

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:40.623
speaking Italian about half the time.

00:15:41.650 --> 00:15:43.200
Yet at the same time that they held on

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:46.200
to these ancient grudges
and secret handshakes,

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:48.960
they encouraged their children,
my father's generation,

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:51.930
to assimilate and lay
claim to the possibilities

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:53.923
afforded by this new country.

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:59.100
Their bookshelves held
inexpensive matched reprint sets

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:02.090
of Hemingway, Steinbeck, and Mark Twain,

00:16:02.090 --> 00:16:05.030
as well as the 20 volumes
of the Book of Knowledge,

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:08.750
with their imposing
embossed ox blood-colored

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:10.493
faux leather bindings.

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:13.840
The children, not just of the Italians,

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:16.120
but the Irish and the Polish Catholics

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:19.210
and the Eastern European Jews took the cue

00:16:19.210 --> 00:16:22.720
and moved out after World
War II, some of them anyway,

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:24.400
from Brooklyn and Queens

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:26.420
to the Long Island post-war suburbs

00:16:26.420 --> 00:16:29.050
where every immigrant
could have the same house

00:16:29.050 --> 00:16:34.050
and bleach their ethnic complexion
to roughly the same hue,

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:36.053
that seemed to be the idea anyway.

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:40.460
So Long Island South Shore,
where my parents settled,

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:42.750
with its endless stretches
of department stores

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:47.040
and Levittown-inspired
tract housing developments,

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:50.790
its yawning sky as yet
uninterrupted by big trees

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:52.713
or interesting-looking buildings,

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:57.000
represented an aggressively
asserted version of normalcy

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:00.300
after the unimaginable
sacrifices and hardships

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:02.570
and heartbreaks of the second World War,

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:05.520
a promised land for many of
those who had lived through it,

00:17:06.870 --> 00:17:09.290
black and white, although
obviously coming down

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:11.300
more heavily on the white side.

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:15.860
But it was an eerily affectless
and rootless landscape

00:17:15.860 --> 00:17:18.300
to a generation of adolescents
who came of age there,

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:19.730
myself included.

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:22.900
Those photos in Jazzmen became for me

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:25.180
a kind of treasure map, a set of clues

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:27.133
to what might be possible in life,

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:30.400
a taproot into a past that
could nourish the present

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:32.860
and future in ways I
could not yet imagine,

00:17:32.860 --> 00:17:34.700
but which I could feel,

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:37.563
like the tangy air of coming springtime.

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:43.813
Two, this is the second raid,
this is a series of raids.

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:47.700
New Orleans is often characterized

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:51.420
as a special case kind of
place within the United States,

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.040
we talked about this a
bit, Joe and Crystal,

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:56.470
and somehow not entirely American.

00:17:56.470 --> 00:18:00.090
Partly European, partly Caribbean,

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:03.670
divorced, or at least amicably separated

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:06.390
from the supposed American
ethos of hard work,

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:10.910
rugged individualism,
and capitalist venality.

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:13.280
This is not entirely inaccurate,

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:17.100
but it is most certainly
incomplete at best.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:18.620
New Orleans is, in my view,

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:21.700
a quintessentially American place,

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:25.120
one which reflects and embodies
the tensions and ironies,

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:28.660
the survival strategies,
and the as yet unhealed

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:33.113
and inadequately addressed
wounds of the nation as a whole.

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:37.440
New Orleans, with its exposed
roots in France, in Spain,

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:41.700
in Africa, has offered a model
not of cultural assimilation,

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:45.400
that mythic and monstrous Melting Pot

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:48.913
that we all hear about, but
rather a cultural polyphony.

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:53.060
The city has fashioned
a collective identity

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:55.770
out of constituent national
and ethnic identities;

00:18:55.770 --> 00:19:00.560
French, Italian, African,
Spanish, German, Irish,

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:03.730
the list is too long to continue.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:06.030
The food, the music, the architecture

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:09.370
are famously amalgams of all the distinct

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:11.300
identifiable ethnic compounds,

00:19:11.300 --> 00:19:14.530
and yet also unmistakably New Orleans.

00:19:14.530 --> 00:19:16.580
This construction of what might be called

00:19:16.580 --> 00:19:18.760
a cultural identity out of the tension

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:23.370
among wildly various
preexisting cultural identities

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:25.403
is nothing if not a paradox,

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:28.750
generating an endless
series of complications

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:32.940
and variations, Moire
patterns, that summon a reality

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:35.430
far more complex and richly textured

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:38.220
than any of their compounded parts,

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:40.150
and I think much the
same thing might be said

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:41.600
about the country as a whole.

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:43.773
Three.

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:48.410
America has never quite settled for itself

00:19:48.410 --> 00:19:50.370
where it stands in
relation to the question

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:54.260
of inherited position,
of whatever variety,

00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:56.533
versus social and cultural mobility.

00:19:57.660 --> 00:20:00.633
The nation as a whole, and
New Orleans in particular,

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:04.340
afford the freedom and
sometimes the necessity

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:09.060
of switching identities,
assuming alternative identities.

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:12.370
At the same time they advance
a counterbalancing emphasis

00:20:12.370 --> 00:20:16.170
on the centrality and
dominance of old families

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:18.280
and other types of lineage.

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:20.740
On the one hand, the injunction
to recreate yourself,

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:24.680
expand your horizons, go West, innovate,

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:27.020
on the other a sharply
articulated awareness

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:30.700
of family background,
class privilege, ethnicity,

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:33.560
race, gender identifiers.

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:35.880
This emphasis is asserted
the more strongly

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:39.080
in proportion to the social
flux amid which it exists,

00:20:39.080 --> 00:20:42.313
and it can be found across
the class and ethnic spectrum.

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:46.400
The DAR, Mayflower Descendants,
The Social Register,

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:49.550
the old-line families of
New Orleans, of Boston,

00:20:49.550 --> 00:20:53.250
of every major city, as well
as all the urgent identifiers

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:55.090
involved in what is usually referred to

00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:59.070
as identity politics and
the abrupt rise and hunger

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:01.800
for DNA evidence of genetic background.

00:21:01.800 --> 00:21:05.633
Ancestry.com should really
be called identity.com.

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:10.410
Does anybody know the story,
Nathaniel Hawthorne's story,

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:12.553
My Kinsman, Major Molineux?

00:21:12.553 --> 00:21:13.770
Anybody know that one?

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:16.120
Oh great, I get to turn
you on to a good story.

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:19.230
Nathaniel Hawthorne's short story,

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:22.710
My Kinsman, Major Molineux,
oh man, you gotta read this,

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:24.360
it's one of the great American stories,

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:27.260
follows a young man from
the countryside named Robin

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:31.090
as he arrives in a
certain little metropolis

00:21:31.090 --> 00:21:33.230
of a New England colony

00:21:33.230 --> 00:21:36.310
in search of a supposedly wealthy relative

00:21:36.310 --> 00:21:38.970
who's gonna set him up,
install him in a position,

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:42.043
and endow him with privilege
that he hasn't earned himself,

00:21:42.043 --> 00:21:43.930
he's very young, he hasn't had a chance

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:47.380
to earn this position, but
he thinks this kinsman of his

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:50.073
is going to hook him up.

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:52.550
This is all fine with our young man,

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:54.950
but as he walks the streets inquiring

00:21:54.950 --> 00:21:57.790
as to where he might find
his kinsman, Major Molineux,

00:21:57.790 --> 00:22:00.930
he is met with hostility and ridicule,

00:22:00.930 --> 00:22:03.340
until finally a wise and helpful spirit

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:05.760
invites Robin to wait
with him on the street

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:08.480
with the promise that Robin's
kinsman will soon pass by,

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:11.660
and he does, the Major does,

00:22:11.660 --> 00:22:16.610
borne on a litter by a wild
mob, got up in crazy garb

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:20.053
with faces painted, with
wild music, by torchlight,

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:23.093
and he's been tarred and feathered.

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:26.060
As the joke sinks in for Robin,

00:22:26.060 --> 00:22:28.810
the folly of leaning too
heavily on the expectation

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:32.250
that his antecedence,
his inherited identity,

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:33.780
will make his way for him

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:35.790
instead of having to
create it for himself,

00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:39.210
his wise companion
enjoins him not to leave

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:42.210
and go back to the country,
but to remain in town

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:44.370
where he, quote, may rise in the world

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:47.093
without the help of your
kinsman, Major Molineux.

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:51.280
Remake yourself, live with the joke,

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:54.160
sustain tension between
the hand you were dealt

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:55.610
and what you make of it.

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:59.690
Improvisation is the word for
making your own variations

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:01.970
upon a set of givens,

00:23:01.970 --> 00:23:05.840
and America has been an
improviser's culture,

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:08.520
nothing if not an improviser's culture.

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:11.700
The nation grew out of the
need to use and transform

00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:14.210
what was to be found here,

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:16.260
in a landscape not just geographical,

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:17.803
but social and economic,

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:21.610
and the culture advances
this myth of improvisation

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:23.540
in countless ways from the comic

00:23:23.540 --> 00:23:25.453
to the tragic end of the spectrum.

00:23:26.510 --> 00:23:28.850
The leaving of home in search of a dream,

00:23:28.850 --> 00:23:31.890
an elusive prize that often
turns out not to be worth it,

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:36.580
or an illusion, as in Bobby
Bare's great performance

00:23:36.580 --> 00:23:39.947
of the song Detroit City,
anybody know that song?

00:23:39.947 --> 00:23:42.260
Ah, look at you guys.

00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:43.670
Okay, I hope you all are taking notes,

00:23:43.670 --> 00:23:45.690
you need to go check this stuff out.

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:49.140
Detroit City, it's great,
it's a song that's sung

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:53.770
in the persona of a guy who
has left his home down south

00:23:53.770 --> 00:23:56.290
and goes up north to work
in the factories in Detroit,

00:23:56.290 --> 00:24:01.160
and he says by day I make the
cars by night I make the bars,

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:06.098
hey, okay, and then there's
this refrain in it of,

00:24:06.098 --> 00:24:08.000
♪ I wanna go home ♪

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:09.879
♪ I wanna go home ♪

00:24:09.879 --> 00:24:13.080
♪ Gee, but I wanna go home ♪

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:14.292
Thank you, anyway.

00:24:14.292 --> 00:24:15.720
(audience laughing)

00:24:15.720 --> 00:24:19.920
But that refrain, of I wanna go home,

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:23.360
lives underneath American song
and literature consistently,

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:26.476
as it became more and more American,

00:24:26.476 --> 00:24:28.970
a chorus underneath all
the restless traveling

00:24:28.970 --> 00:24:33.930
and optimistic bluster, it's
there as an ostinato of sorts.

00:24:33.930 --> 00:24:36.610
The voyage may be the traveling
north from a South Shore

00:24:36.610 --> 00:24:39.230
to an opportunity, or
the leaving of the East

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:41.090
with its establishment norms

00:24:41.090 --> 00:24:42.770
heading for the frontier of the West

00:24:42.770 --> 00:24:45.590
with all of its possibilities
and its dangers,

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:47.800
or it could be Nick Carraway

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:49.380
leaving the Midwest for New York

00:24:49.380 --> 00:24:52.960
with all of its own Gatsby-esque
promises and dangers,

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:57.380
the old paradigm so often to
be found in European novels,

00:24:57.380 --> 00:24:59.540
certainly of the provincial
coming to the city

00:24:59.540 --> 00:25:02.750
to make his way upward,
literally and figuratively,

00:25:02.750 --> 00:25:05.380
a novel saved from an excessive reliance

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:09.120
on the European model by the
resoundingly symbolic invention

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:11.363
of Gatsby, with his insatiable hunger.

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:15.510
Our two greatest American
novels contain multitudes;

00:25:15.510 --> 00:25:17.070
Huckleberry Finn, of course,

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:19.480
with its improvising protagonist

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:23.140
who just wanted to go somewhere,
he weren't particular,

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:27.320
and his encounter with the grand
array of riverfront con men

00:25:27.320 --> 00:25:30.210
and masqueraders in the company of Jim,

00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:33.830
an enslaved person who is making
his own gamble on freedom,

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:38.830
and whose fate is inextricably
bound up with Huck's.

00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:42.360
And then there's Moby-Dick,
with its Pequod populated

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:45.350
by every imaginable
nationality and ethnic group

00:25:45.350 --> 00:25:49.430
presided over by Ahab and
his monomaniacal quest

00:25:49.430 --> 00:25:52.940
for something unattainable;
domination over nature,

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:56.980
over life itself, a
mania that leads to doom.

00:25:56.980 --> 00:26:00.210
In Twain's book everything turns out okay,

00:26:00.210 --> 00:26:02.580
and in Melville's book everything ends

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:04.780
in shards and splinters.

00:26:04.780 --> 00:26:06.850
Which vision prevails?

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:08.783
We hang suspended between both.

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:11.313
Four.

00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:15.540
The novelist Ralph Ellison once remarked

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:20.520
that "The search for identity
is the American theme."

00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:24.910
He gave that search a form
in his novel Invisible Man,

00:26:24.910 --> 00:26:27.310
which we talked about today in class,

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:31.070
in which the narrator and
central character lacks a name

00:26:31.070 --> 00:26:34.150
as well as a fixed identity, excuse me,

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:36.290
and is constantly looking to other people

00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:38.933
and external situations to lend him one.

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:43.213
And yet the word search
feels problematic to me,

00:26:44.310 --> 00:26:49.310
with its implicit positioning
of identity as a fixed goal,

00:26:49.460 --> 00:26:54.460
something findable,
preexisting, awaiting discovery.

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:57.230
The novel's protagonist
never does disembark

00:26:57.230 --> 00:26:59.260
at some safe harbor of a found,

00:26:59.260 --> 00:27:03.190
or even a constructed identity, after all,

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:08.050
only calls a supposedly
temporary truce with that goal,

00:27:08.050 --> 00:27:09.900
as if on the next try he might emerge

00:27:09.900 --> 00:27:13.360
from his underground hole
and finally grasp it,

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:17.150
create the uncreated features of his face,

00:27:17.150 --> 00:27:20.400
in his twist on Stephen Dedalus' desire

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:23.870
to create the uncreated
conscience of his race.

00:27:23.870 --> 00:27:26.630
But perhaps the idea of that search itself

00:27:26.630 --> 00:27:31.363
is not just elusive, but a
mistake, an error in perception.

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:36.120
Perhaps identity in the American sense,

00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:38.610
the broadest American sense, involves

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:42.870
not so much the search for,
much less the discovery of,

00:27:42.870 --> 00:27:45.890
the single preexisting role awaiting one,

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:49.840
some final secure off-the-rack identity,

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:52.360
or even the creation of
a single definitive face

00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:55.360
for oneself, as it does
the constant transformation

00:27:55.360 --> 00:28:00.360
of the given; the conversion
of objects into subjects,

00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:05.823
the act of creative argument
with one's own assumptions.

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:09.110
I would say that identity is to be located

00:28:09.110 --> 00:28:12.320
in the nature and quality
of that transformation,

00:28:12.320 --> 00:28:15.080
that conversion, that argument,

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:19.940
a dynamic engagement with
it all, a kind of hologram

00:28:19.940 --> 00:28:23.063
created by energies pointed
in different directions.

00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:27.960
In an essay on Bob Dylan,

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:30.430
who I think is one of
the most representative

00:28:30.430 --> 00:28:33.960
American artists who's ever done it,

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:36.690
in my collection Devil Sent the Rain,

00:28:36.690 --> 00:28:40.000
I said I wasn't an essayist,
but it's an essay collection,

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:41.413
it's also got some journals,

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:45.670
examining the extraordinary
variety of American utterance

00:28:45.670 --> 00:28:50.670
and reference to which Dylan
laid, and lays still, claim,

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:54.780
I tripped on the word
American, and the word America,

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:57.210
a couple times and I wrote this;

00:28:57.210 --> 00:29:00.630
America, meaning what, again?

00:29:00.630 --> 00:29:02.640
Cowboys and Indians?

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:05.050
New York City, Hollywood?

00:29:05.050 --> 00:29:08.020
The Civil War, the CIA,

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:11.850
interstate highways, Main Street, Walmart?

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:13.120
John Wayne?

00:29:13.120 --> 00:29:15.240
John Wayne Gacy?

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:19.010
Earl Scruggs, Mohammed Ali,
Rosa Parks, Don Rickles,

00:29:19.010 --> 00:29:21.220
Flaco Jimenez, Edmund Wilson?

00:29:21.220 --> 00:29:23.860
Redwood forests, Gulf Stream waters?

00:29:23.860 --> 00:29:27.000
Ellis Island, Los Alamos?

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:29.530
America wants to include all possibility,

00:29:29.530 --> 00:29:32.980
hence it takes up potentially
all the space there is,

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:35.140
it expands.

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:37.130
Something so internally contradictory

00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:41.530
is of course a target for
every kind of projection.

00:29:41.530 --> 00:29:44.550
The mind has trouble accepting
such intense contradictions

00:29:44.550 --> 00:29:46.520
within the same entity,

00:29:46.520 --> 00:29:49.990
their presence creates a profound anxiety.

00:29:49.990 --> 00:29:52.020
Learning to accept them is a discipline,

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:53.840
to identify with the culture itself

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:56.160
means identifying with a high level

00:29:56.160 --> 00:29:57.950
of tension among elements,

00:29:57.950 --> 00:30:00.477
it means identifying
with the tension itself.

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:03.653
Five.

00:30:06.920 --> 00:30:08.570
It's gonna be a long lecture, so.

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:12.663
Five, in The Birth of Tragedy,

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:16.490
Frederick Nietzsche sets
out his famous notion

00:30:16.490 --> 00:30:19.910
of what he calls the
Apollonian and Dionysian

00:30:19.910 --> 00:30:22.350
aspects of human nature,

00:30:22.350 --> 00:30:25.020
positing a fruitful tension between them.

00:30:25.020 --> 00:30:27.510
Roughly, and somewhat oversimply put,

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:29.250
the Apollonian tendencies are those

00:30:29.250 --> 00:30:31.540
that pull in the direction
of order, measure,

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:33.573
hierarchy, and individuation,

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:36.780
and the Dionysian, or
Di-o-nee-sian aspects

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:40.810
represent the tendency toward
dissolution of a distinct self

00:30:40.810 --> 00:30:42.980
in communal orgiastic activity

00:30:42.980 --> 00:30:44.980
and altered states that exist,

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:48.210
to use his phrase, beyond good and evil.

00:30:48.210 --> 00:30:51.150
On one side, morals, ethics, moderation,

00:30:51.150 --> 00:30:54.220
on the other, impulse,
excess, unregulated passion.

00:30:54.220 --> 00:30:56.750
He also states, if I read him correctly,

00:30:56.750 --> 00:30:58.860
that the two sets of impulses exist

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:01.613
in a kind of corrective
relationship, one to the other.

00:31:03.050 --> 00:31:04.890
American culture has always struggled

00:31:04.890 --> 00:31:07.710
to maintain its balance
on this fault line,

00:31:07.710 --> 00:31:10.430
like a tightrope walker
constantly making micro,

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:13.323
and sometimes macro corrections.

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:17.200
If any single place in the United States

00:31:17.200 --> 00:31:19.870
distills the essence of
the contrast and tension

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:22.720
between the Apollonian and the Dionysian,

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:24.790
it would be New Orleans,

00:31:24.790 --> 00:31:27.850
and never more fully
than at Mardi Gras time,

00:31:27.850 --> 00:31:29.993
when the entire community almost,

00:31:30.900 --> 00:31:33.400
participates in both
the Dionysian loosening

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:37.000
of moral ligature, in
masking, drinking, eating,

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:39.380
and doing all the other
stuff we know about,

00:31:39.380 --> 00:31:42.350
and at the same time the
Apollonian reenactments

00:31:42.350 --> 00:31:44.900
of traditions and pageantry

00:31:44.900 --> 00:31:47.380
that go back well into the 19th century,

00:31:47.380 --> 00:31:50.020
and really in some cases before that,

00:31:50.020 --> 00:31:52.140
often in detail scripted down

00:31:52.140 --> 00:31:54.373
to the smallest hand gesture, literally.

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:59.290
Whether we see bank
presidents who wear hoods

00:31:59.290 --> 00:32:02.490
unhappily reminiscent of Klan outfits

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:06.750
riding on lavishly decorated
floats in the Krewe of Rex,

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:09.850
the always-white King of Carnival,

00:32:09.850 --> 00:32:13.770
or the grand mirroring mockery
of the 100-plus year old

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:16.140
African American social club Zulu

00:32:17.330 --> 00:32:20.500
with its own king and
court consisting primarily

00:32:20.500 --> 00:32:22.970
of the city's black bourgeois elite

00:32:22.970 --> 00:32:25.890
dressed in parody, bitter parody,

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:27.930
of supposedly African garb;

00:32:27.930 --> 00:32:30.940
grass skirts and blackened faces,

00:32:30.940 --> 00:32:33.370
or the Mardi Gras
Indians, made up primarily

00:32:33.370 --> 00:32:36.240
of working class African
Americans who roam the streets

00:32:36.240 --> 00:32:38.490
wearing extraordinary handmade suits

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:41.610
and singing songs and
performing ritualized gestures

00:32:41.610 --> 00:32:44.290
that go back, again, well
into the 19th century,

00:32:44.290 --> 00:32:47.630
is the mask that endows the individual

00:32:47.630 --> 00:32:50.090
with the ability to transcend the given

00:32:50.090 --> 00:32:53.620
and emerge into the
possibility of transformation.

00:32:53.620 --> 00:32:56.400
In either case, this
transformation is sanctioned,

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:59.100
understood as a necessary
element in managing

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:02.210
and mediating energies that
might otherwise break loose

00:33:02.210 --> 00:33:04.603
and do lasting damage to the social order.

00:33:05.530 --> 00:33:08.640
This disillusion of the day to day self,

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:11.150
or rather the substitution of another self

00:33:11.150 --> 00:33:13.940
that exempts one from the
usual responsibilities

00:33:13.940 --> 00:33:17.570
and guidelines of social
standing, propriety, station,

00:33:17.570 --> 00:33:21.050
location, affiliation, is
understood by all concerned

00:33:21.050 --> 00:33:24.973
to be an assumed identity,
a mask that transforms you.

00:33:26.790 --> 00:33:27.623
Six.

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:31.900
While all this is recognizable

00:33:31.900 --> 00:33:33.950
as characteristically New Orleans,

00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:35.860
we should also understand that masking

00:33:35.860 --> 00:33:39.630
is not just a perennial,
but a defining element

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:42.300
of American culture as a whole.

00:33:42.300 --> 00:33:44.700
It's of course true that masking

00:33:44.700 --> 00:33:46.200
is part of the cultural fabric

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:47.920
in most places in the world

00:33:47.920 --> 00:33:50.603
in ritual enactment and celebration,

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:54.320
but it has served important
additional purposes here

00:33:54.320 --> 00:33:57.270
in our mythology-drenched
cultural landscape,

00:33:57.270 --> 00:33:59.340
where social position,
(clears throat) excuse me,

00:33:59.340 --> 00:34:01.230
ethnic background, gender identity,

00:34:01.230 --> 00:34:03.863
even the mass appetite for team sports,

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:07.850
may be understood as a form of masking,

00:34:07.850 --> 00:34:09.763
a shadow play of identity.

00:34:10.650 --> 00:34:13.900
It has been a way of encoding
or channeling aspiration,

00:34:13.900 --> 00:34:17.440
doubt, guilt, even unspeakable desire,

00:34:17.440 --> 00:34:19.700
and has often in itself
served as a passport

00:34:19.700 --> 00:34:21.770
to social mobility.

00:34:21.770 --> 00:34:25.840
Members of marginalized groups
have often found it necessary

00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:29.113
or desirable to become
virtuosos of the mask;

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:32.620
whether Jewish Americans who
have passed for gentiles,

00:34:32.620 --> 00:34:34.610
or African Americans
with skin light enough

00:34:34.610 --> 00:34:36.870
to allow them to, quote, pass for white,

00:34:36.870 --> 00:34:39.830
in both cases is a way of
opening doors of opportunity

00:34:39.830 --> 00:34:42.160
for themselves and their families

00:34:42.160 --> 00:34:44.613
that might otherwise have remained closed.

00:34:45.540 --> 00:34:49.230
The masquerade can go
both ways, of course.

00:34:49.230 --> 00:34:51.660
The foundation of a recognizable

00:34:51.660 --> 00:34:53.950
indigenous American musical performance

00:34:53.950 --> 00:34:58.470
was blackface minstrelsy,
the most peculiar institution

00:34:58.470 --> 00:35:01.090
of American culture in 19th century,

00:35:01.090 --> 00:35:03.723
and well into the 20th, be it said,

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:07.670
the rock and roll of its time, in fact.

00:35:07.670 --> 00:35:10.800
White performers blackened
their faces with burnt cork

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:13.460
and performed stylized and often,

00:35:13.460 --> 00:35:16.780
as I said this in class,
viciously caricatured impressions

00:35:16.780 --> 00:35:21.750
of supposed black types,
whether devious or carefree,

00:35:21.750 --> 00:35:25.443
and scenes of idyllic life
on the old plantation.

00:35:26.720 --> 00:35:29.410
This entertainment found
its first mass audience

00:35:29.410 --> 00:35:31.800
in the north, where the scenes of blacks

00:35:31.800 --> 00:35:34.250
portrayed as either
simple-minded or criminal

00:35:34.250 --> 00:35:36.880
eased the collective white mind somewhat

00:35:36.880 --> 00:35:40.263
about being complicit in
the perpetuation of slavery.

00:35:41.780 --> 00:35:43.960
It would be nearly impossible to overstate

00:35:43.960 --> 00:35:45.760
minstrelsy's influence on the course

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:48.510
of American popular entertainment.

00:35:48.510 --> 00:35:51.640
Sam Philips, the charismatic founder

00:35:51.640 --> 00:35:55.020
of the legendary Memphis
record label, record company

00:35:55.020 --> 00:35:59.897
Sun Records, said sometime
around 1954 or 1955,

00:35:59.897 --> 00:36:02.657
"Find me a white man who
can sing like a negro

00:36:02.657 --> 00:36:04.637
"and I'll make a million dollars."

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:07.900
And when he discovered Elvis Presley

00:36:07.900 --> 00:36:10.763
he made his million, and more, and more.

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:14.910
But 100 years before
that, white men singing

00:36:14.910 --> 00:36:19.910
supposedly like negros dominated
the entertainment world.

00:36:19.950 --> 00:36:23.000
The assuming of the mask
of blackness endures today,

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:25.110
in every white suburban high school kid

00:36:25.110 --> 00:36:28.370
who imitates the gestures and
accents of rappers, homie,

00:36:28.370 --> 00:36:32.330
that mask of blackness allowing
the expression of attitudes

00:36:32.330 --> 00:36:35.600
and modes of expression
otherwise considered unacceptable

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:39.313
in so-called proper, i.e. white, society.

00:36:40.740 --> 00:36:44.050
But blackface performance
was not the only form

00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:47.350
of ethnic masking that became
wildly popular in the U.S.,

00:36:47.350 --> 00:36:48.940
far from it.

00:36:48.940 --> 00:36:52.220
The early 20th century, with
its mass influx of immigrants

00:36:52.220 --> 00:36:55.570
from southern and eastern
Europe was a rich time

00:36:55.570 --> 00:36:57.050
for ethnic impersonation.

00:36:57.050 --> 00:36:59.950
Vaudeville drew upon a
gallery of stock characters

00:36:59.950 --> 00:37:02.340
based on ethnic stereotypes,

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:05.320
and in fact provided an entrance ramp

00:37:05.320 --> 00:37:08.270
from which so many German,
Irish, Jewish immigrants

00:37:08.270 --> 00:37:11.180
made their way into the American
entertainment mainstream

00:37:11.180 --> 00:37:14.060
like cars entering a
fast-moving interstate.

00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:17.320
Jewish performers especially
have been ballet masters

00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:18.693
of this kind of masking.

00:37:19.810 --> 00:37:22.540
Often this entree actually
came from carrying on

00:37:22.540 --> 00:37:26.340
the minstrel tradition, as
the singer Al Jolson did

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:30.110
in full blackface singing
songs like Mammy both onstage

00:37:30.110 --> 00:37:33.120
and in the first popular talking picture,

00:37:33.120 --> 00:37:36.180
The Jazz Singer, released in 1927,

00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:38.780
songwriter Irving Berlin, who was Jewish,

00:37:38.780 --> 00:37:40.740
providing a mostly Christian nation

00:37:40.740 --> 00:37:43.423
with its holiday anthem White Christmas,

00:37:44.310 --> 00:37:48.180
all the way to Bob Dylan,
born Robert Zimmerman,

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:50.560
Bob Dylan's appropriation
of everything he liked,

00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:53.620
from ancient Anglo-Irish
ballads through country blues,

00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:56.930
rock and roll, lyric poetry, and more.

00:37:56.930 --> 00:37:59.900
Saul Bellow once remarked
that you were entitled

00:37:59.900 --> 00:38:02.460
to steal anything in writing

00:38:02.460 --> 00:38:04.783
that you were strong enough to carry out.

00:38:06.210 --> 00:38:08.730
Freedom suggests itself as the readiness

00:38:08.730 --> 00:38:11.313
to adopt as many masks as possible.

00:38:13.230 --> 00:38:14.063
Seven.

00:38:15.830 --> 00:38:18.270
New Orleans has been
called the land of dreams

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:20.310
in songs like Basin Street Blues

00:38:20.310 --> 00:38:23.090
and Way Down Yonder in New Orleans,

00:38:23.090 --> 00:38:26.500
but has anyone ever defined
that much-abused formulation,

00:38:26.500 --> 00:38:27.883
the American Dream?

00:38:28.820 --> 00:38:31.400
A thing devoutly to be wished, apparently,

00:38:31.400 --> 00:38:34.380
a protean set of possibilities
that everyone defines

00:38:34.380 --> 00:38:38.290
for himself or herself,
out of what an uneasy sleep

00:38:38.290 --> 00:38:39.903
such a dream might come.

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:43.810
In any organized social context

00:38:43.810 --> 00:38:46.430
one leads a double life, at least,

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:49.593
as both a private individual
and a member of the society.

00:38:50.540 --> 00:38:52.810
In the United States
each individual carries

00:38:52.810 --> 00:38:54.430
the weight of responsibility

00:38:54.430 --> 00:38:58.110
for the fate of the
democratic enterprise itself,

00:38:58.110 --> 00:39:03.110
this noble, flawed experiment
in which we are engaged.

00:39:03.300 --> 00:39:07.090
Huckleberry Finn tries to
escape it for most of a novel,

00:39:07.090 --> 00:39:10.380
but is finally forced into
assuming responsibility

00:39:10.380 --> 00:39:13.060
for his choice regarding
the fate of his friend,

00:39:13.060 --> 00:39:15.150
the escaped slave Jim.

00:39:15.150 --> 00:39:17.370
In Moby-Dick the men on the Pequod

00:39:17.370 --> 00:39:19.690
maintain their varied individual aspects

00:39:19.690 --> 00:39:22.240
while performing their duties as seamen,

00:39:22.240 --> 00:39:26.130
their limited responsibility
for the collective enterprise,

00:39:26.130 --> 00:39:28.860
as that enterprise is hijacked

00:39:28.860 --> 00:39:32.640
by the unstable genius Captain Ahab,

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:35.490
a commander in chief driven by revenge

00:39:35.490 --> 00:39:40.330
walking the deck alone at all
hours, accepting no council,

00:39:40.330 --> 00:39:42.930
tweeting from his cabin in
the middle of the night.

00:39:42.930 --> 00:39:46.103
Oops, sorry, I got my commanders
mixed up for a moment.

00:39:47.940 --> 00:39:50.450
To me, New Orleans is the American Dream

00:39:50.450 --> 00:39:53.080
writ large in Dayglo colors,

00:39:53.080 --> 00:39:55.490
and it takes the word dream literally

00:39:55.490 --> 00:39:57.790
in that it advances in symbolic form

00:39:57.790 --> 00:40:01.660
images meant to embody
not just possibilities,

00:40:01.660 --> 00:40:03.940
hidden yearnings, hungers, and needs,

00:40:03.940 --> 00:40:08.470
but also warnings of danger
perhaps too deep and threatening

00:40:08.470 --> 00:40:09.743
to face straight on.

00:40:11.020 --> 00:40:13.990
What could be more
dream-like, more symbolic,

00:40:13.990 --> 00:40:17.040
and more troublingly
American than the uproar

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:19.280
around the removal of the stone effigies

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:22.070
of Jefferson Davis and Robert E. Lee

00:40:22.070 --> 00:40:24.100
from the perches where they were set up

00:40:24.100 --> 00:40:27.830
over a century ago to remind the citizenry

00:40:27.830 --> 00:40:31.120
of a white supremacy that seemed
lost during Reconstruction,

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:33.530
but that had been reclaimed quickly

00:40:33.530 --> 00:40:37.490
by the oh-so-brave men
riding masked in hoods

00:40:37.490 --> 00:40:40.120
to reassert white dominance.

00:40:40.120 --> 00:40:42.200
What could be more
dream-like than individuals

00:40:42.200 --> 00:40:45.290
from inside and outside the
city watching the removals

00:40:45.290 --> 00:40:49.340
dressed in Confederate flag bandanas

00:40:49.340 --> 00:40:51.300
and Yes We Can t-shirts

00:40:51.300 --> 00:40:53.230
with Barack Obama's likeness on them,

00:40:53.230 --> 00:40:56.040
youths in Army fatigue
jackets with peace symbols,

00:40:56.040 --> 00:40:58.500
and motorcycle guys with denim vests

00:40:58.500 --> 00:41:00.690
carrying automatic weapons.

00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:04.040
You saw all of it if you
were there, which I was.

00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:06.680
You would think that the Civil
War had never been settled

00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:10.140
and that the fate of the Union
still hangs in the balance,

00:41:10.140 --> 00:41:12.543
and it does, it does.

00:41:13.730 --> 00:41:16.800
We've entered a time now
in which the word American

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:21.460
has been hijacked by thieves
in suits and flag lapel pins

00:41:21.460 --> 00:41:24.020
who assert that America
means white people,

00:41:24.020 --> 00:41:26.770
not people of color, straight
nuclear family people,

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:29.910
not gay or transgender people,
certainly not immigrants,

00:41:29.910 --> 00:41:31.540
while they rob the country blind

00:41:31.540 --> 00:41:34.300
under the cloak of law and order

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:37.070
with a sociopathic
six-year-old at the helm,

00:41:37.070 --> 00:41:39.603
madder by far than Captain Ahab,

00:41:41.030 --> 00:41:43.650
attempting to push large
groups of Americans

00:41:43.650 --> 00:41:46.170
and aspiring Americans to the margins,

00:41:46.170 --> 00:41:49.450
often by executive orders
that would write them out

00:41:49.450 --> 00:41:51.570
of the very definition of American;

00:41:51.570 --> 00:41:53.860
black lives, women's lives,

00:41:53.860 --> 00:41:55.960
lives of the appropriately-named Dreamers,

00:41:55.960 --> 00:41:58.910
all of whom find it necessary to insist

00:41:58.910 --> 00:42:01.310
as a means of their very survival

00:42:01.310 --> 00:42:03.173
on their distinct identities.

00:42:04.330 --> 00:42:07.400
Turning and turning in the widening gyre,

00:42:07.400 --> 00:42:09.540
the falcon cannot hear the falconer,

00:42:09.540 --> 00:42:12.670
things fall apart, the center cannot hold,

00:42:12.670 --> 00:42:15.440
mere anarchy is loosed upon the world,

00:42:15.440 --> 00:42:17.970
wrote Yeates, of course,
in The Second Coming,

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:20.480
and perhaps it is not mere anarchy,

00:42:20.480 --> 00:42:24.970
but a carefully crafted shadow
play of mass distraction

00:42:24.970 --> 00:42:27.570
allowing a small percentage
of vested brigands

00:42:27.570 --> 00:42:29.083
to steal the silver and gold.

00:42:31.360 --> 00:42:33.610
In the introduction to
Devil Sent the Rain,

00:42:33.610 --> 00:42:36.310
my essay collection,
speaking of developments

00:42:36.310 --> 00:42:39.640
during the George W. Bush administration

00:42:39.640 --> 00:42:41.963
I wrote the following;

00:42:43.060 --> 00:42:46.610
The divisions that were being
wedged more and more deeply

00:42:46.610 --> 00:42:49.310
into the national dialogue
were psychological,

00:42:49.310 --> 00:42:51.490
even mythological.

00:42:51.490 --> 00:42:55.050
What were once differences
of policy and even philosophy

00:42:55.050 --> 00:42:58.750
had become coated with
magic and superstition.

00:42:58.750 --> 00:43:01.370
Manipulations cunningly crafted to bypass

00:43:01.370 --> 00:43:04.450
the reasoning faculty
and go right to the heart

00:43:04.450 --> 00:43:08.230
of the fear and uncertainty
that were there under the crust,

00:43:08.230 --> 00:43:11.080
like rich oil reserves
that could be tapped

00:43:11.080 --> 00:43:14.150
to fuel a brutal expansion.

00:43:14.150 --> 00:43:17.400
The question became whether
there was an opposing magic

00:43:17.400 --> 00:43:21.370
to counteract the enormous
undertow of all that imagery,

00:43:21.370 --> 00:43:25.900
all that appealed to easy,
comforting answers, end quote.

00:43:25.900 --> 00:43:29.050
And that was written about a
time 10 years ago and more,

00:43:29.050 --> 00:43:32.700
amid a landscape of discourse
that was downright athenian

00:43:32.700 --> 00:43:34.943
compared to the slop bucket we're in now.

00:43:36.380 --> 00:43:37.593
Is there a cure?

00:43:38.620 --> 00:43:40.700
Must things fall apart?

00:43:40.700 --> 00:43:43.330
I asked myself that
question after Katrina,

00:43:43.330 --> 00:43:48.330
revisiting the broken
city, my home that I loved,

00:43:48.690 --> 00:43:51.003
and that many wanted to write off,

00:43:51.840 --> 00:43:53.653
but that needed to be fought for.

00:43:54.490 --> 00:43:57.300
Could New Orleans offer
not just a dream warning

00:43:57.300 --> 00:44:00.193
for the nation, but a dream
hint of a way forward?

00:44:02.760 --> 00:44:03.593
Eight.

00:44:05.530 --> 00:44:08.850
I hit my stride as a fiction writer,

00:44:08.850 --> 00:44:10.650
and maybe as an improviser,

00:44:10.650 --> 00:44:13.793
on my first trip to New Orleans in 1987.

00:44:14.730 --> 00:44:16.660
I had landed in town to spend a week

00:44:16.660 --> 00:44:19.330
trying to recover from a broken heart,

00:44:19.330 --> 00:44:21.610
and to attend the Jazz
and Heritage Festival

00:44:21.610 --> 00:44:24.520
which I had wanted to do for years.

00:44:24.520 --> 00:44:26.910
On arrival I found that my accommodations

00:44:26.910 --> 00:44:30.960
were going to be different than I thought;

00:44:30.960 --> 00:44:33.330
I was either going to have to share a bed

00:44:33.330 --> 00:44:35.890
with the proprietor of the guest house

00:44:35.890 --> 00:44:38.630
that I had booked sight unseen

00:44:38.630 --> 00:44:40.230
from some sketchy advertisement.

00:44:40.230 --> 00:44:41.960
I should say, this is before,

00:44:41.960 --> 00:44:45.530
for those of you undergraduate age,

00:44:45.530 --> 00:44:46.840
this was before the internet,

00:44:46.840 --> 00:44:51.380
this was before Airbnb, HomeAway, Expedia,

00:44:51.380 --> 00:44:52.700
whatever it might be, this was like,

00:44:52.700 --> 00:44:54.180
you looked in advertisements

00:44:54.180 --> 00:44:55.990
and it was like closing your eyes

00:44:55.990 --> 00:44:58.350
and throwing a dart, really.

00:44:58.350 --> 00:45:00.443
Which though proffered as an option,

00:45:02.188 --> 00:45:04.580
what I mean, sharing the
bed with the proprietor

00:45:04.580 --> 00:45:06.750
was not one as far as I was concerned,

00:45:06.750 --> 00:45:09.550
or to sleep on a folding
cot in the kitchen

00:45:09.550 --> 00:45:11.350
where I had spotted several cockroaches

00:45:11.350 --> 00:45:15.752
that resembled good sized
cigar butts, only ambulatory.

00:45:15.752 --> 00:45:17.450
(audience laughing)

00:45:17.450 --> 00:45:19.390
I took a moment or three to wrap my brain

00:45:19.390 --> 00:45:20.450
around the situation.

00:45:20.450 --> 00:45:24.490
This was not the New Orleans
groove that I had imagined,

00:45:24.490 --> 00:45:28.390
but as if one set of assumptions
were being slid off of me

00:45:29.450 --> 00:45:34.240
like a decal and another
slid into place, I thought,

00:45:34.240 --> 00:45:37.960
no man, you wanted to come to New Orleans,

00:45:37.960 --> 00:45:41.250
this is what it is, this is
the hand you've been dealt,

00:45:41.250 --> 00:45:43.710
deal with it or don't deal with it.

00:45:43.710 --> 00:45:46.040
And I thought I'll deal with it,

00:45:46.040 --> 00:45:48.770
so I slept in the kitchen,
and the next night

00:45:48.770 --> 00:45:51.863
a bonafide guest room
opened up, as if by magic.

00:45:53.100 --> 00:45:55.150
But for me, the project of writing,

00:45:55.150 --> 00:45:56.840
I'm sorry, the process of writing a novel

00:45:56.840 --> 00:45:58.820
is very much like this.

00:45:58.820 --> 00:46:01.240
You have one set of ideas when you embark,

00:46:01.240 --> 00:46:05.240
and a provisional sense of
the map of the book, maybe,

00:46:05.240 --> 00:46:07.600
but as you get into it and
find out that certain things

00:46:07.600 --> 00:46:10.650
are less possible than
you thought they were,

00:46:10.650 --> 00:46:12.950
and characters reveal
aspects of themselves

00:46:12.950 --> 00:46:16.740
that you didn't anticipate,
you have to go with that,

00:46:16.740 --> 00:46:20.760
you have to improvise a
response to the unforeseen,

00:46:20.760 --> 00:46:23.190
and out of a series of such responses

00:46:23.190 --> 00:46:26.840
your book begins to find its identity.

00:46:26.840 --> 00:46:29.680
I don't know that all
writers work this way,

00:46:29.680 --> 00:46:31.470
in fact I'm sure they don't,

00:46:31.470 --> 00:46:34.623
but I do think that most
literary writers probably do.

00:46:35.610 --> 00:46:38.990
Was it just a total coincidence
that I found my first voice

00:46:38.990 --> 00:46:42.270
as a fiction writer on my
first visit to New Orleans?

00:46:42.270 --> 00:46:45.640
I don't know, maybe, but I
sat at the Napoleon House

00:46:45.640 --> 00:46:48.220
and wrote the first draft of
my first good short story,

00:46:48.220 --> 00:46:51.730
Brownsville, by the way, that
was the first one I wrote

00:46:51.730 --> 00:46:54.313
where I really actually felt coordinated,

00:46:56.860 --> 00:46:59.240
wrote the first draft of my
first short story Brownsville,

00:46:59.240 --> 00:47:01.890
and in fact when I sent
it in to Gordon Lish,

00:47:01.890 --> 00:47:05.180
great editor, legendary
editor Gordon Lish,

00:47:05.180 --> 00:47:08.010
used to add fiction for
Esquire and other places,

00:47:08.010 --> 00:47:11.050
when I sent it in to Gordon
Lish's magazine The Quarterly,

00:47:11.050 --> 00:47:15.220
I sent it in under a
pseudonym, an assumed identity,

00:47:15.220 --> 00:47:17.580
a mask, if you will.

00:47:17.580 --> 00:47:19.800
I will not tell you what it is

00:47:19.800 --> 00:47:21.813
because I might need it again someday,

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:25.820
leave it that I had literally found

00:47:25.820 --> 00:47:27.790
another way to be myself,

00:47:27.790 --> 00:47:30.483
if self is even really the right word.

00:47:32.040 --> 00:47:34.220
But New Orleans would
teach me many more lessons

00:47:34.220 --> 00:47:35.100
in years to come.

00:47:35.100 --> 00:47:39.000
Some lessons were welcome,
and some were not,

00:47:39.000 --> 00:47:41.480
but even the unwelcome
ones proved to be useful

00:47:41.480 --> 00:47:43.600
as I improvised my responses,

00:47:43.600 --> 00:47:47.030
and one of the lessons,
maybe the most important,

00:47:47.030 --> 00:47:50.180
was that rarely is anything
exactly what it seems

00:47:50.180 --> 00:47:51.920
on first glance.

00:47:51.920 --> 00:47:54.300
So let me tell you a
little story about that

00:47:54.300 --> 00:47:55.613
to close out this lecture.

00:47:57.850 --> 00:48:01.970
Shortly after I arrived
in New Orleans to live,

00:48:01.970 --> 00:48:05.440
which was in 1994, after years of visiting

00:48:05.440 --> 00:48:06.670
every chance I could find,

00:48:06.670 --> 00:48:09.510
I made the acquaintance of
a Mardi Gras Indian chief

00:48:09.510 --> 00:48:11.260
named Donald Harrison.

00:48:11.260 --> 00:48:14.420
Big Chief Donald Harrison,
somebody's nodding their head,

00:48:14.420 --> 00:48:16.720
Big Chief of the Guardians
of the Flame Tribe.

00:48:19.720 --> 00:48:22.050
Can I reasonably assume
that most of you know

00:48:22.050 --> 00:48:25.100
what Mardi Gras Indians were,
by the way, I should ask,

00:48:25.100 --> 00:48:27.510
raise your hand if you
don't know, I wanna see.

00:48:27.510 --> 00:48:30.810
If you don't know, okay,
alright, don't be afraid,

00:48:30.810 --> 00:48:32.730
okay good, everybody knows, terrific.

00:48:32.730 --> 00:48:36.750
So Donald Harrison was the
chief of this particular gang,

00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:39.420
he's also the father of the
wonderful jazz saxophonist

00:48:39.420 --> 00:48:40.783
Donald Harrison, Jr.

00:48:42.420 --> 00:48:45.340
Anyway, so you know,
the Mardi Gras Indians,

00:48:45.340 --> 00:48:47.870
gangs of African Americans
who spend most of every year

00:48:47.870 --> 00:48:50.170
sewing elaborate feathered
costumes that they wear

00:48:50.170 --> 00:48:52.330
for the first time on Mardi Gras day,

00:48:52.330 --> 00:48:54.380
and then only a couple
more times afterward,

00:48:54.380 --> 00:48:57.440
and making a new one the
next year and so forth.

00:48:57.440 --> 00:48:59.730
The tradition goes well
back into the 19th century,

00:48:59.730 --> 00:49:03.010
as I said before, their visual
impact is often overwhelming,

00:49:03.010 --> 00:49:05.000
as is the sound of their chanted songs

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:07.180
and rhythmic accompaniment.

00:49:07.180 --> 00:49:09.510
I wish we had some film
footage to show right now,

00:49:09.510 --> 00:49:11.950
but there's plenty in Treme for sure.

00:49:11.950 --> 00:49:16.010
I no longer remember exactly
how I met Chief Donald,

00:49:16.010 --> 00:49:17.380
although I had seen him dressed

00:49:17.380 --> 00:49:20.190
in one of his astonishing
suits, assemblages,

00:49:20.190 --> 00:49:23.170
like all Mardi Gras Indian
suits, consisting of feathers

00:49:23.170 --> 00:49:26.410
and beaded pectorial
patches sewn onto canvas,

00:49:26.410 --> 00:49:28.730
and jewels in geometric arrangements,

00:49:28.730 --> 00:49:31.853
explosions of glorious
invention and grandeur.

00:49:33.040 --> 00:49:34.260
Whatever the circumstances,

00:49:34.260 --> 00:49:35.740
I must have expressed an interest

00:49:35.740 --> 00:49:38.490
in talking to him about
the Indians, and he,

00:49:38.490 --> 00:49:41.880
with a very characteristic New
Orleans generosity of spirit,

00:49:41.880 --> 00:49:44.730
invited me to meet him for a
drink at a bar in the Treme,

00:49:45.840 --> 00:49:48.250
by some accounts the oldest
continuously occupied

00:49:48.250 --> 00:49:50.520
African American neighborhood
in North America,

00:49:50.520 --> 00:49:52.430
in New Orleans, just across Rampart Street

00:49:52.430 --> 00:49:55.980
from the French Quarter,
it's where it begins.

00:49:55.980 --> 00:49:59.280
So I showed up and waited for
him for about half an hour

00:49:59.280 --> 00:50:02.530
until through the bar's gloomy half light

00:50:02.530 --> 00:50:05.690
I saw him approaching,
a very different figure

00:50:05.690 --> 00:50:07.610
from the one I had last seen decked out

00:50:07.610 --> 00:50:10.410
in powder blue plumes and feathers,

00:50:10.410 --> 00:50:13.710
engaged in a verbal duel
with some other Indian chief.

00:50:13.710 --> 00:50:17.170
This was a trim man in a very natty jacket

00:50:17.170 --> 00:50:20.350
and stingy brim pork
pie hat, he greeted me

00:50:20.350 --> 00:50:23.150
and we fell easily into conversation,

00:50:23.150 --> 00:50:25.680
and this is how I described the encounter

00:50:25.680 --> 00:50:27.350
in my book Why New Orleans Matters,

00:50:27.350 --> 00:50:29.960
rather than try to strain
to recast it for y'all,

00:50:29.960 --> 00:50:31.630
just to tell you how it goes.

00:50:31.630 --> 00:50:34.030
I said we chatted for a
while but this and that,

00:50:34.920 --> 00:50:36.550
he asked me what kind of work I did

00:50:36.550 --> 00:50:38.770
and I told him that I write books.

00:50:38.770 --> 00:50:42.130
He asked me what kind and
I told him both fiction

00:50:42.130 --> 00:50:46.900
and nonfiction, and that I
wrote a lot about music as well.

00:50:46.900 --> 00:50:48.027
I asked him if he liked to read,

00:50:48.027 --> 00:50:49.833
and he told me he did.

00:50:50.790 --> 00:50:52.697
When I asked him what he
liked to read he said,

00:50:52.697 --> 00:50:56.037
"Mainly philosophy and some fiction.

00:50:56.037 --> 00:50:58.597
"I have an extensive library
of books on philosophy

00:50:58.597 --> 00:51:01.107
"and African American history as well."

00:51:02.030 --> 00:51:03.463
This surprised me.

00:51:04.450 --> 00:51:08.790
I thought I knew what and who
the Mardi Gras Indians were,

00:51:08.790 --> 00:51:10.700
and I was surprised to hear this man,

00:51:10.700 --> 00:51:14.170
whom I had seen dressed in
elaborate Indian regalia,

00:51:14.170 --> 00:51:16.160
plumes and feathers sprouting,

00:51:16.160 --> 00:51:19.410
chanting African-based rhythms
over tambourine background,

00:51:19.410 --> 00:51:21.770
say that he read philosophy.

00:51:21.770 --> 00:51:25.520
To my shame I had a moment of skepticism

00:51:25.520 --> 00:51:27.590
that lay beneath my next question,

00:51:27.590 --> 00:51:30.773
which was which philosophers
do you like to read?

00:51:31.617 --> 00:51:34.897
"Lately," he said,
"mainly Bertrand Russell,

00:51:34.897 --> 00:51:36.400
"but I love Nietzsche too."

00:51:38.640 --> 00:51:41.330
It's an old American situation of course,

00:51:41.330 --> 00:51:44.420
anyone interested in an
extended essay on the subject

00:51:44.420 --> 00:51:48.720
should read Ralph Ellison's The
Little Man at Chehaw Station

00:51:48.720 --> 00:51:51.350
in his collection Going to the Territory,

00:51:51.350 --> 00:51:54.570
or Constance Rourke's
Seminole book-length study,

00:51:54.570 --> 00:51:55.730
American Humor.

00:51:55.730 --> 00:51:57.780
By the way, I wanna
recommend that book to you,

00:51:57.780 --> 00:52:00.660
it's dated in some respects,
it came out in 1931.

00:52:00.660 --> 00:52:04.930
Constance Rourke is one of
the most unjustly neglected

00:52:04.930 --> 00:52:08.710
cultural historians who's
ever done that thing,

00:52:08.710 --> 00:52:11.613
and her book American
Humor is indispensable.

00:52:12.490 --> 00:52:14.590
In fact, I learned about
it from Ralph Ellison

00:52:14.590 --> 00:52:16.063
and his friend Albert Murray,

00:52:17.070 --> 00:52:21.663
also a Tuskegee-educated
novelist, a nonfiction writer.

00:52:22.560 --> 00:52:25.910
Anyway, both of these
books extended meditations

00:52:25.910 --> 00:52:29.720
on the masks not just
that we put on for others,

00:52:29.720 --> 00:52:32.003
but that we put on others.

00:52:33.100 --> 00:52:35.740
It's a lesson that one
has to learn continually

00:52:35.740 --> 00:52:37.940
in New Orleans, it's the end of the quote,

00:52:37.940 --> 00:52:41.560
and, I would add, in
our lives as Americans,

00:52:41.560 --> 00:52:42.710
improvising our way

00:52:42.710 --> 00:52:45.360
through a continuously-evolving landscape

00:52:45.360 --> 00:52:48.810
of possibility and identity.

00:52:48.810 --> 00:52:50.310
And I'm gonna leave it there,

00:52:50.310 --> 00:52:54.080
and now I'll improvise
some responses to questions

00:52:54.080 --> 00:52:55.690
or remarks if you have any.

00:52:55.690 --> 00:52:58.247
I wanna thank you very
much for your attention.

00:52:58.247 --> 00:53:01.414
(audience applauding)

00:53:03.906 --> 00:53:04.739
Thank you.

00:53:07.540 --> 00:53:11.630
Any questions, any remarks,
any balls to serve me

00:53:11.630 --> 00:53:13.663
over the net, I'll hit it back if I can.

00:53:16.040 --> 00:53:16.960
Okay, yes?

00:53:16.960 --> 00:53:18.353
- [Audience Member]
What is your connection

00:53:18.353 --> 00:53:21.963
between jazz and the act of writing?

00:53:21.963 --> 00:53:24.850
- Ah, the question if
you couldn't hear it was

00:53:24.850 --> 00:53:26.860
what's my connection between jazz

00:53:26.860 --> 00:53:28.603
and the act of writing you said?

00:53:30.330 --> 00:53:32.580
Well, there are probably
a few different ways

00:53:32.580 --> 00:53:33.733
I could answer that,

00:53:35.690 --> 00:53:39.270
and they all probably go back
to the idea of improvisation,

00:53:39.270 --> 00:53:40.910
although I don't consider jazz

00:53:40.910 --> 00:53:43.170
to be strictly an improvisatory form,

00:53:43.170 --> 00:53:47.090
I think improvisation
is inextricably bound up

00:53:47.090 --> 00:53:48.073
with jazz music.

00:53:49.190 --> 00:53:53.640
For me, I can answer maybe
in two or three parts.

00:53:53.640 --> 00:53:58.640
For one thing, my notion of
the process of generating

00:54:00.620 --> 00:54:04.320
a fictional narrative
is very improvisatory

00:54:04.320 --> 00:54:07.540
to which I usually have some
kind of provisional sense

00:54:07.540 --> 00:54:10.900
when I start out of what
the cord changes will be.

00:54:10.900 --> 00:54:12.700
By the way, the person
who asked this question

00:54:12.700 --> 00:54:13.880
is a dear old friend of mine

00:54:13.880 --> 00:54:16.510
who I have not seen for probably 40 years,

00:54:16.510 --> 00:54:19.410
Brian Torff, who's one of the
finest American bass players,

00:54:19.410 --> 00:54:21.960
great jazz bass player, and other genres,

00:54:21.960 --> 00:54:24.180
so that's where that question comes from.

00:54:24.180 --> 00:54:26.910
So the cord changes in effect,

00:54:26.910 --> 00:54:29.450
one might have a sense that one's gonna,

00:54:29.450 --> 00:54:31.450
one thinks one's gonna start out playing

00:54:32.350 --> 00:54:34.430
I'll Remember April and you find out

00:54:34.430 --> 00:54:36.083
you were really playing Stardust,

00:54:37.100 --> 00:54:39.740
but you have to get into
the text to find that out.

00:54:39.740 --> 00:54:42.760
So the process of composition entails

00:54:42.760 --> 00:54:45.860
constantly being alive to the things

00:54:45.860 --> 00:54:48.380
that are being suggested
to you by the text

00:54:48.380 --> 00:54:50.243
that you've already generated,

00:54:51.334 --> 00:54:52.850
so it's improvisatory in that sense.

00:54:52.850 --> 00:54:54.850
And then of course one has the luxury,

00:54:54.850 --> 00:54:57.690
if one's a fiction writer,
of going back and re-writing,

00:54:57.690 --> 00:54:59.840
which of course Sonny
Rollins doesn't have,

00:55:02.720 --> 00:55:03.850
so there's that.

00:55:03.850 --> 00:55:08.850
Second of all, and I guess
I'm gonna have to paraphrase

00:55:09.380 --> 00:55:11.840
something I was saying in
response to one of the students

00:55:11.840 --> 00:55:13.530
in the class this afternoon,

00:55:13.530 --> 00:55:18.530
I experience the writing of prose

00:55:20.800 --> 00:55:25.680
in a musical way, and even
in a choreographic way

00:55:25.680 --> 00:55:28.580
in a sense, which is
that one is dealing with

00:55:28.580 --> 00:55:30.553
the manipulation,

00:55:31.610 --> 00:55:34.050
I don't like the overtone
series of that particular word,

00:55:34.050 --> 00:55:36.940
but one's dealing yes,
with the manipulation

00:55:36.940 --> 00:55:40.830
of the passage of time,
and the nature of sequence,

00:55:40.830 --> 00:55:45.000
and the fact of sequence,
which is every bit as important

00:55:45.000 --> 00:55:48.720
in writing fiction as it
is in five card stud poker

00:55:48.720 --> 00:55:50.630
where you roll the cards one at a time.

00:55:50.630 --> 00:55:52.930
You're trying to set up expectations

00:55:52.930 --> 00:55:57.190
and then either subvert
them, or satisfy them

00:55:57.190 --> 00:55:59.960
in some kind of surprising
and agreeable way,

00:55:59.960 --> 00:56:01.860
and coherent way.

00:56:01.860 --> 00:56:03.510
Well, the same thing could be said

00:56:03.510 --> 00:56:07.060
about playing a jazz solo,
or playing in a jazz group,

00:56:07.060 --> 00:56:10.550
but specifically with the sentences

00:56:10.550 --> 00:56:12.943
and the progress of paragraphs,

00:56:14.160 --> 00:56:19.160
one can spend an entire
Ulysses on One Day in Dublin,

00:56:20.020 --> 00:56:25.020
or one can also go through
30 years in a paragraph,

00:56:26.200 --> 00:56:29.793
so that is the manipulation of time.

00:56:31.913 --> 00:56:33.500
There's a great performance
by John Coltrane,

00:56:33.500 --> 00:56:35.900
any jazz fans in the house,
people like jazz here?

00:56:35.900 --> 00:56:37.150
Couple, two people?

00:56:37.150 --> 00:56:38.810
Don't be ashamed to raise your hands,

00:56:38.810 --> 00:56:40.410
stick up for jazz music,

00:56:40.410 --> 00:56:42.570
well there's a great
recording by John Coltrane

00:56:42.570 --> 00:56:44.600
called Blue Train, it's
the title of an album

00:56:44.600 --> 00:56:46.630
and of a track on there, it's a blues,

00:56:46.630 --> 00:56:51.250
and in it Trane, Coltrane,
at certain places

00:56:51.250 --> 00:56:53.860
would be playing many, many notes over,

00:56:53.860 --> 00:56:56.670
it's a walking tempo blues,
about like this maybe,

00:56:56.670 --> 00:56:59.390
right about there, and
sometimes he's playing melodies

00:56:59.390 --> 00:57:00.407
that go (mimicking quick
jazz saxophone music),

00:57:03.000 --> 00:57:05.610
sort of like that, maybe 32nd
notes or whatever they are,

00:57:05.610 --> 00:57:08.920
and then he'll start playing
eighth notes and quarter notes,

00:57:08.920 --> 00:57:12.600
and it's as if, it's again a
metaphor I used in class today,

00:57:12.600 --> 00:57:13.493
but it's as if at a certain point

00:57:13.493 --> 00:57:16.290
there's stuff going by
a train window so fast

00:57:16.290 --> 00:57:18.340
that it's a blur, and
then suddenly you come out

00:57:18.340 --> 00:57:20.690
into open country and you
see a farmhouse, whatever.

00:57:20.690 --> 00:57:25.510
So I can't help that kind
of synesthetic dimension

00:57:25.510 --> 00:57:29.120
of writing prose, narrative
prose specifically,

00:57:29.120 --> 00:57:32.730
and I find it actually
a very tenable analog

00:57:32.730 --> 00:57:34.280
to playing jazz.

00:57:34.280 --> 00:57:35.891
Does that answer the question?

00:57:35.891 --> 00:57:38.610
(laughs) Okay, man, sure.

00:57:38.610 --> 00:57:39.443
Who else?

00:57:41.340 --> 00:57:43.040
Do I have to ask myself questions?

00:57:43.040 --> 00:57:43.873
Okay, go 'head, sure.

00:57:43.873 --> 00:57:45.639
- [Audience Member] At
face value this was great,

00:57:45.639 --> 00:57:47.151
and (voice trailing off).

00:57:47.151 --> 00:57:48.895
But I wanna set you a problem,

00:57:48.895 --> 00:57:51.003
and you can refuse it, but--

00:57:51.003 --> 00:57:52.887
- Hmm, that's quite a lead in, okay.

00:57:52.887 --> 00:57:55.190
(audience laughing)

00:57:55.190 --> 00:57:57.478
- [Audience Member] Well,
it's about improvisation,

00:57:57.478 --> 00:58:00.811
and Treme is such a romantic association

00:58:01.940 --> 00:58:03.437
for a particular neighborhood,

00:58:03.437 --> 00:58:07.383
but New Orleans is a conglomeration
of many neighborhoods,

00:58:07.383 --> 00:58:10.950
and I wonder what would
happen if we set the cast

00:58:10.950 --> 00:58:13.100
just throughout other neighborhoods,

00:58:13.100 --> 00:58:14.980
and if different storylines,

00:58:14.980 --> 00:58:17.630
because it's a multi-plotted series

00:58:17.630 --> 00:58:21.090
of all kinds of different
places activated,

00:58:21.090 --> 00:58:24.150
but what a different emphasis it would be

00:58:24.150 --> 00:58:26.283
if the series were called Bywater,

00:58:29.070 --> 00:58:32.533
or Midtown, or Faubourg Marigny.

00:58:33.543 --> 00:58:36.281
(laughing)

00:58:36.281 --> 00:58:39.371
But just improvise on
shifting the emphasis

00:58:39.371 --> 00:58:40.912
on how the storylines would change

00:58:40.912 --> 00:58:42.843
because of those neighborhoods,

00:58:42.843 --> 00:58:46.173
valuing the fact that it's
not just one place, it's many.

00:58:47.450 --> 00:58:48.670
- What an interesting question,

00:58:48.670 --> 00:58:50.360
did everybody hear it, basically?

00:58:50.360 --> 00:58:53.160
Okay what if, you heard it, alright,

00:58:53.160 --> 00:58:54.650
so I won't try to recapitulate it,

00:58:54.650 --> 00:58:57.373
basically what if the series were called,

00:58:58.260 --> 00:59:01.403
named for some other
neighborhood in New Orleans,

00:59:03.410 --> 00:59:06.910
even though Treme takes
in many, many different,

00:59:06.910 --> 00:59:08.710
the series addressed itself

00:59:08.710 --> 00:59:11.500
to many different places in New Orleans,

00:59:11.500 --> 00:59:15.010
what if the accent were placed
on a different syllable,

00:59:15.010 --> 00:59:17.613
and it were named for a
different neighborhood?

00:59:19.370 --> 00:59:21.003
I think, in a way...

00:59:26.460 --> 00:59:28.870
Well, what is a title in the first place?

00:59:28.870 --> 00:59:33.430
A title serves some kind of
a God function, doesn't it?

00:59:33.430 --> 00:59:38.430
In that a title is related to a text

00:59:38.790 --> 00:59:42.330
the way God is related to
creation, in one way at least,

00:59:42.330 --> 00:59:47.260
which is to say it addresses
itself, or sheds light upon,

00:59:47.260 --> 00:59:51.510
looks over the entire
scope of the narrative

00:59:51.510 --> 00:59:53.270
that it applies to.

00:59:53.270 --> 00:59:56.560
So in that sense, a title functions

00:59:56.560 --> 00:59:58.830
almost like a bit of a creation myth,

00:59:58.830 --> 01:00:03.440
so you're saying here is the
root of meaning in this text.

01:00:03.440 --> 01:00:08.440
So if you were to title
the series Bywater,

01:00:11.700 --> 01:00:13.470
right away you're writing about something,

01:00:13.470 --> 01:00:15.750
now it may take in every neighborhood,

01:00:15.750 --> 01:00:17.960
but you're saying the
wellspring of meaning

01:00:17.960 --> 01:00:21.823
is the experience of people
who, and institutions,

01:00:23.423 --> 01:00:26.760
that are peculiar to this
area, to this neighborhood.

01:00:26.760 --> 01:00:31.210
If you were to title it French
Quarter, that's been done,

01:00:31.210 --> 01:00:34.850
or if you were to title
it any one of, Lower Nine,

01:00:34.850 --> 01:00:39.000
pardon me, sorry, or if you
were to title it Gentilly,

01:00:39.000 --> 01:00:42.030
each one of these titles would suggest

01:00:42.030 --> 01:00:45.920
that somehow the experience or the fact

01:00:45.920 --> 01:00:50.800
of this particular lineage of experience

01:00:50.800 --> 01:00:53.330
is somehow going to cast a light

01:00:53.330 --> 01:00:54.980
that encompasses everything else,

01:00:54.980 --> 01:00:57.650
so you'd have a completely
different creation myth,

01:00:57.650 --> 01:00:58.483
in a sense.

01:00:58.483 --> 01:01:01.673
Now with Treme, and I
did not title the series,

01:01:02.810 --> 01:01:04.460
and I've never been convinced

01:01:04.460 --> 01:01:06.600
that it was necessarily absolutely

01:01:06.600 --> 01:01:10.340
the ideal title for the
series, but whatever.

01:01:10.340 --> 01:01:12.550
For one thing, because
people kept asking me

01:01:12.550 --> 01:01:13.773
if I wrote for Treem.

01:01:14.966 --> 01:01:15.799
(audience laughing)

01:01:15.799 --> 01:01:18.100
Is that a title, is that a soda?

01:01:18.100 --> 01:01:23.100
Anyway, so whether it was
the ideal title or not,

01:01:25.330 --> 01:01:26.700
I think what it was saying,

01:01:26.700 --> 01:01:30.990
being that it was this
historically ancient, really,

01:01:30.990 --> 01:01:34.010
African American settlement, neighborhood,

01:01:34.010 --> 01:01:37.280
within New Orleans, it is saying something

01:01:37.280 --> 01:01:41.100
about African American
culture in New Orleans

01:01:41.100 --> 01:01:43.490
as being foundational to the city,

01:01:43.490 --> 01:01:48.090
and fructifying to all
the different narratives

01:01:48.090 --> 01:01:50.070
in all parts of the city.

01:01:50.070 --> 01:01:52.640
That may be an impossibly
romantic view of it,

01:01:52.640 --> 01:01:55.734
but I don't think it's
completely off the wall.

01:01:55.734 --> 01:01:57.060
And I think that's what would change,

01:01:57.060 --> 01:01:59.610
I don't wanna go through
all the different possible

01:02:01.390 --> 01:02:04.610
parameters, permutations
of, but that's, I think,

01:02:04.610 --> 01:02:05.443
how it would change,

01:02:05.443 --> 01:02:07.200
that's the nature of how it would change,

01:02:07.200 --> 01:02:08.390
that's an improvised answer.

01:02:08.390 --> 01:02:10.980
Ha, it was alright? Okay.

01:02:10.980 --> 01:02:12.740
Thanks Jerry, yeah, Jeff?

01:02:12.740 --> 01:02:14.160
- [Jeff] This is great, thank you for,

01:02:14.160 --> 01:02:15.253
- Sure, no, no, go.

01:02:18.159 --> 01:02:19.662
- [Jeff] When I think about,

01:02:19.662 --> 01:02:21.580
so I love, sticking with improvisation,

01:02:21.580 --> 01:02:23.840
and I guess maybe I just want
you to say a little more,

01:02:23.840 --> 01:02:26.036
improvise a little
more, for improvisation,

01:02:26.036 --> 01:02:27.374
I know you've been (garbled speaking),

01:02:27.374 --> 01:02:31.370
and when I saw the title
as an occupational hazard,

01:02:31.370 --> 01:02:33.630
the two phenomenon that I thought of

01:02:33.630 --> 01:02:35.100
were sex and teaching.

01:02:35.100 --> 01:02:36.750
- Were what?
- Sex and teaching.

01:02:38.339 --> 01:02:40.490
- Two things that shouldn't
be combined a whole lot,

01:02:40.490 --> 01:02:42.250
necessarily.
- Except I think of,

01:02:42.250 --> 01:02:44.535
as you said, certain jazz is not entirely

01:02:44.535 --> 01:02:47.550
an improvisatory format, though
that's true about (mumbles),

01:02:47.550 --> 01:02:49.486
that contradiction in terms
is true of everything,

01:02:49.486 --> 01:02:51.110
there's always some kind of form,

01:02:51.110 --> 01:02:53.820
teaching is just sort
of endlessly exhausting

01:02:53.820 --> 01:02:56.460
improvising sometimes, and of course sex,

01:02:56.460 --> 01:03:01.460
but also there's a form that
outside of a certain range

01:03:01.780 --> 01:03:04.810
is no longer teaching,
and it's no longer sex,

01:03:04.810 --> 01:03:06.747
it's sexual assault, or
it's whatever else it is.

01:03:06.747 --> 01:03:08.580
And so I guess, whatever,
I'm going on a tangent,

01:03:08.580 --> 01:03:11.044
but the point is, I wonder, one,

01:03:11.044 --> 01:03:12.610
what exactly you mean by improvising,

01:03:12.610 --> 01:03:15.037
what is masking an example of in the talk,

01:03:15.037 --> 01:03:18.840
and what do you think
of as a kind of form of

01:03:18.840 --> 01:03:20.983
improvisation when it comes to that?

01:03:20.983 --> 01:03:22.770
- Okay, it's a multipart question,

01:03:22.770 --> 01:03:26.150
so what are some examples
of masking in the talk.

01:03:26.150 --> 01:03:29.103
- [Jeff] What is masking just
an example of in the talk,

01:03:29.103 --> 01:03:32.230
because I take it improvisational
as a kind of liquidity

01:03:32.230 --> 01:03:34.520
to identity that you're
searching or locating

01:03:34.520 --> 01:03:39.520
and finding identity, and
so is masking an example,

01:03:40.230 --> 01:03:43.820
the good kind of masking an
example of improvising identity?

01:03:43.820 --> 01:03:46.700
- Well, I think it always, masking I think

01:03:46.700 --> 01:03:50.610
probably always entails
an injunction to improvise

01:03:52.170 --> 01:03:53.780
because you're going to find yourself

01:03:53.780 --> 01:03:58.510
in an unaccustomed situation.

01:03:58.510 --> 01:04:01.930
I mean, unless it's the same
mask you wear every week,

01:04:01.930 --> 01:04:03.650
but even then, even then.

01:04:03.650 --> 01:04:06.810
Now, of course we find ourselves
in unscripted situations

01:04:06.810 --> 01:04:09.560
even when we're not, quote,
unquote, wearing a mask,

01:04:09.560 --> 01:04:13.220
but a lotta times a mask is put on you,

01:04:13.220 --> 01:04:16.200
we're constantly measuring the temperature

01:04:16.200 --> 01:04:19.220
of any situation, the slant
of light in any situation,

01:04:19.220 --> 01:04:20.470
how does this person see me,

01:04:20.470 --> 01:04:22.550
what are the assumptions this person has?

01:04:22.550 --> 01:04:25.133
I walk into this room as a person

01:04:25.133 --> 01:04:28.560
that I have my own understanding,
maybe, provisional,

01:04:28.560 --> 01:04:29.910
of who I am, I walk into this room

01:04:29.910 --> 01:04:32.540
and it's a bunch of people
who are going to have

01:04:32.540 --> 01:04:34.360
certain class assumptions about me,

01:04:34.360 --> 01:04:36.780
or certain ethnic assumptions.

01:04:36.780 --> 01:04:39.930
So we're constantly
improvising our responses

01:04:39.930 --> 01:04:40.900
to these situations.

01:04:40.900 --> 01:04:44.300
One of the things that masks can do

01:04:44.300 --> 01:04:48.450
is allow you to control
that a little bit more.

01:04:48.450 --> 01:04:52.720
Like okay, I understand
what this Frankenstein mask

01:04:52.720 --> 01:04:55.380
is gonna say to a number of people,

01:04:55.380 --> 01:05:00.060
I see they're gonna see it as a quotation

01:05:00.060 --> 01:05:05.040
about a monster movie, so they
can be relating to the mask

01:05:05.040 --> 01:05:07.020
while I'm doing something else,

01:05:07.020 --> 01:05:09.960
this is one of the great
advantages of masking.

01:05:09.960 --> 01:05:12.810
For instance, you could
have a president, let's say,

01:05:12.810 --> 01:05:16.603
who was a complete swindler, deadbeat,

01:05:17.852 --> 01:05:20.223
really mentally deficient,

01:05:20.223 --> 01:05:23.230
and really out to bilk everybody

01:05:23.230 --> 01:05:26.440
from every last dollar
that they have if he could,

01:05:26.440 --> 01:05:30.160
but who presents himself
as a great populist.

01:05:30.160 --> 01:05:32.610
Hey, you know these
guys, blah, blah, blah,

01:05:32.610 --> 01:05:35.850
so people who want to see that mask,

01:05:35.850 --> 01:05:39.873
you can use that mask to
manipulate and steal the silver,

01:05:40.920 --> 01:05:44.170
and we're living through
that now, in my opinion.

01:05:44.170 --> 01:05:48.140
But a mask, in other words,
I think allows you to

01:05:49.200 --> 01:05:54.180
control the circumstances of
the improvisation, in a way,

01:05:54.180 --> 01:05:57.000
and maybe to a degree that
you wouldn't be able to

01:05:57.000 --> 01:06:00.100
if you just came out as
yourself, because after all,

01:06:00.100 --> 01:06:02.510
even our sense of our own
selves is very fragmented

01:06:02.510 --> 01:06:04.180
most of the time.

01:06:04.180 --> 01:06:07.270
We talk very glibly
about who we are, myself,

01:06:07.270 --> 01:06:09.320
this is who I, this is my identity.

01:06:09.320 --> 01:06:13.150
Your identity is about
12 or 14 different things

01:06:13.150 --> 01:06:16.200
at any given time anyway,
you're one person,

01:06:16.200 --> 01:06:18.497
this is a dull thing to
say, it's so obvious,

01:06:18.497 --> 01:06:20.930
and said so often, but
we're different people

01:06:20.930 --> 01:06:23.763
with our parents, with our
friends, lovers, whatever.

01:06:24.840 --> 01:06:29.613
So the nature of improvisation
to me is just presence,

01:06:31.610 --> 01:06:35.790
finally, and a quality of
alertness to the implications

01:06:35.790 --> 01:06:39.140
of what is being dealt you at that time.

01:06:39.140 --> 01:06:41.410
Now, were you saying you
wanted an example of masking

01:06:41.410 --> 01:06:42.330
from the--
- No, no.

01:06:42.330 --> 01:06:44.430
- Oh, alright, okay, but
does this make sense,

01:06:44.430 --> 01:06:45.860
what I'm saying, does
that adequately address

01:06:45.860 --> 01:06:46.960
what you were raising?

01:06:47.870 --> 01:06:48.750
Will, did you have something

01:06:48.750 --> 01:06:50.180
you were gonna ask?
- Yeah, I was just saying,

01:06:50.180 --> 01:06:53.730
this reminds me a lot of an exhibit I saw

01:06:53.730 --> 01:06:58.164
at the Jazz Museum, it was
part of the P4 Eve this year,

01:06:58.164 --> 01:07:00.060
it was called The Lotus
In Spite of the Swamp,

01:07:00.060 --> 01:07:02.910
but it was actually an exhibit
on the Mardi Gras Indians

01:07:02.910 --> 01:07:06.583
and at the beginning
they had a short poem by,

01:07:07.460 --> 01:07:10.460
it's Kalamu Ya Salaam from the Ninth Ward,

01:07:10.460 --> 01:07:12.970
and just a quote that he had is that

01:07:12.970 --> 01:07:16.219
New Orleans is both and
rather than either/or,

01:07:16.219 --> 01:07:18.790
and so I felt like this
resonates with your talk.

01:07:19.930 --> 01:07:22.450
And I was just wondering, do you think,

01:07:22.450 --> 01:07:23.630
you might not be able
to answer this question,

01:07:23.630 --> 01:07:26.400
but my question is what
do you think the source

01:07:26.400 --> 01:07:28.310
of that identity is in New Orleans,

01:07:28.310 --> 01:07:31.010
do we think it's just
an accident of history,

01:07:31.010 --> 01:07:34.790
the fact that in many ways the
oldest people in New Orleans

01:07:34.790 --> 01:07:37.360
are not the Americans, but the Creoles,

01:07:37.360 --> 01:07:39.240
and then also in southern
Louisiana the Cajuns,

01:07:39.240 --> 01:07:40.910
and the people from the
Caribbean, et cetera,

01:07:40.910 --> 01:07:44.210
and now for to be the truest New Orleanian

01:07:44.210 --> 01:07:46.870
is not necessarily be the trust American,

01:07:46.870 --> 01:07:49.850
or is it something that
has developed uniquely

01:07:49.850 --> 01:07:51.223
in the subsequent years?

01:07:53.996 --> 01:07:57.113
- What a question, or,
yeah, it's a question.

01:07:58.700 --> 01:08:01.940
There's so many things that are germane

01:08:01.940 --> 01:08:05.320
to New Orleans history where you could say

01:08:05.320 --> 01:08:07.000
well, here's the historical reason

01:08:07.000 --> 01:08:09.190
why this kind of thing
developed in this way,

01:08:09.190 --> 01:08:13.620
and I don't have enough of
the actual factual history

01:08:13.620 --> 01:08:16.110
at my fingertips where I could say well,

01:08:16.110 --> 01:08:17.030
here was the thing,

01:08:17.030 --> 01:08:19.920
when the Germans settled
this neighborhood,

01:08:19.920 --> 01:08:23.363
so I can't approach the
question from that angle.

01:08:24.300 --> 01:08:27.060
What I can say is that first and foremost,

01:08:27.060 --> 01:08:29.820
first and foremost, above everything else,

01:08:29.820 --> 01:08:31.213
New Orleans is a port.

01:08:32.660 --> 01:08:34.560
So any time you have a port you have

01:08:34.560 --> 01:08:38.930
an extraordinarily
heterogeneous population

01:08:38.930 --> 01:08:42.870
that is engaged in a lot of
different kinds of activity,

01:08:42.870 --> 01:08:45.010
some of which is very opportunistic,

01:08:45.010 --> 01:08:49.280
some of which is exploratory,
people are leaving,

01:08:49.280 --> 01:08:51.520
people are coming in who are strangers,

01:08:51.520 --> 01:08:53.130
you've got all the ingredients

01:08:53.130 --> 01:08:56.090
of every kind of story right there.

01:08:56.090 --> 01:08:59.500
The departure on an adventure,
on a voyage of discovery,

01:08:59.500 --> 01:09:01.680
the arrival in town of the stranger,

01:09:01.680 --> 01:09:03.300
you've got it all mixed together,

01:09:03.300 --> 01:09:06.710
so all of these narratives,

01:09:06.710 --> 01:09:08.540
these different types of narratives,

01:09:08.540 --> 01:09:13.190
are mixing together in an active port

01:09:13.190 --> 01:09:15.170
to a degree that they don't, let's say,

01:09:15.170 --> 01:09:20.170
in the mythic small town, in
which there's a certain stasis

01:09:20.500 --> 01:09:22.830
and then it's disturbed a
little bit by this or that

01:09:22.830 --> 01:09:25.150
irregularity of whatever sort.

01:09:25.150 --> 01:09:27.820
So New Orleans, first of all,

01:09:27.820 --> 01:09:30.910
even without anything
else going on, is a port,

01:09:30.910 --> 01:09:35.910
and it has all that implied
injunction to improvisation

01:09:36.260 --> 01:09:38.310
based on all these (mic falling),

01:09:38.310 --> 01:09:40.265
can I please get rid of this thing?

01:09:40.265 --> 01:09:42.703
- It's for the video.
- What is this?

01:09:42.703 --> 01:09:45.060
- [Man] I think it's
the mic for the video.

01:09:45.060 --> 01:09:48.510
- Yeah, I think maybe we--
- It has to be there, okay.

01:09:48.510 --> 01:09:50.240
Hey, how you doing, alright.

01:09:50.240 --> 01:09:53.260
So what was I talking about?

01:09:53.260 --> 01:09:54.093
Oh yeah...

01:09:56.670 --> 01:09:59.793
So it's an improvisatory
situation to begin with,

01:10:01.240 --> 01:10:05.190
but especially because of the,

01:10:05.190 --> 01:10:10.190
first of all it had this
peculiar double quality

01:10:10.360 --> 01:10:14.360
of being one of the most
active slave markets

01:10:14.360 --> 01:10:18.230
that there was, especially
for a certain period,

01:10:18.230 --> 01:10:21.870
and at the same time
having a huge population

01:10:21.870 --> 01:10:24.960
of free people of color, so right away

01:10:24.960 --> 01:10:28.390
you're in a kind of hall
of mirrors right away.

01:10:28.390 --> 01:10:32.060
Then you had neighborhoods
that were settled successively

01:10:33.769 --> 01:10:37.090
by the French, by the
Spanish, we know this,

01:10:37.090 --> 01:10:39.540
other neighborhoods that
were pockets of Italian,

01:10:41.222 --> 01:10:44.250
New York City shares some of that quality,

01:10:44.250 --> 01:10:48.680
so you have all that
mixture of cultural richness

01:10:48.680 --> 01:10:53.640
and also mutual suspicion,
and cultural blending,

01:10:53.640 --> 01:10:55.360
all of that stuff that comes from having

01:10:55.360 --> 01:11:00.360
a very diverse population
in any situation,

01:11:01.100 --> 01:11:03.830
so all of that is there.

01:11:03.830 --> 01:11:07.640
Also, and I could go on and on about this,

01:11:07.640 --> 01:11:10.320
but one of the other
things about New Orleans

01:11:10.320 --> 01:11:14.370
was that specifically
African, and Afro-Caribbean,

01:11:14.370 --> 01:11:18.830
but specifically African
cultural elements were,

01:11:18.830 --> 01:11:23.830
maybe by an accident of
civic law or something,

01:11:23.950 --> 01:11:28.410
allowed to be expressed publicly

01:11:28.410 --> 01:11:30.710
in a way that they weren't
in a lot of places.

01:11:31.720 --> 01:11:34.140
Many of you have probably
heard of Congo Square,

01:11:34.140 --> 01:11:35.900
which was this area in New Orleans

01:11:35.900 --> 01:11:40.010
right on one side between
Basin and Rampart Street,

01:11:40.010 --> 01:11:44.950
where enslaved people
could go and play drums,

01:11:44.950 --> 01:11:47.970
African drumming, outside, and dance,

01:11:47.970 --> 01:11:52.260
this was back even in the 18th
century, the 19th century,

01:11:52.260 --> 01:11:57.260
so what the ethnographers
sometimes call African retentions

01:11:57.460 --> 01:12:01.370
were just out there in plain sight,

01:12:01.370 --> 01:12:05.760
and were an animating,
supremely animating,

01:12:05.760 --> 01:12:08.120
dimension of the city's cultural life,

01:12:08.120 --> 01:12:10.710
from way back and way back.

01:12:10.710 --> 01:12:13.680
So I guess I'm losing the
thread of the question

01:12:13.680 --> 01:12:16.590
maybe a little bit, but I
take it that you're asking me

01:12:19.880 --> 01:12:22.720
why is New Orleans such a
diverse place or something,

01:12:22.720 --> 01:12:27.560
why is there such an injunction
to improvisation there?

01:12:27.560 --> 01:12:30.720
But I think any time that
you have all that cultural,

01:12:30.720 --> 01:12:35.090
historical, ethnic, whatever
you wanna say, contrast

01:12:35.090 --> 01:12:37.510
you're gonna have that, and
then specifically add to that

01:12:37.510 --> 01:12:39.050
the port city dimension,

01:12:39.050 --> 01:12:42.300
and the fact that it was a
French colony for a long time,

01:12:42.300 --> 01:12:46.980
that too gave this somewhat
different casts, many reasons.

01:12:46.980 --> 01:12:49.490
I suggest if you wanna
read an excellent book

01:12:49.490 --> 01:12:51.260
about New Orleans history,

01:12:51.260 --> 01:12:54.010
especially the early part
of New Orleans history,

01:12:54.010 --> 01:12:57.040
Tulane professor Lawrence
Powell wrote a book

01:12:57.040 --> 01:13:00.390
called The Accidental
City, and that's, I think,

01:13:00.390 --> 01:13:02.974
probably the best history
of early New Orleans

01:13:02.974 --> 01:13:05.670
that you could read, and
if you haven't read it,

01:13:05.670 --> 01:13:07.425
especially you being from New Orleans,

01:13:07.425 --> 01:13:08.913
you might wanna check that out.

01:13:10.560 --> 01:13:11.408
Yes.

01:13:11.408 --> 01:13:12.373
- [Joe] I think that'll
be the last question.

01:13:12.373 --> 01:13:14.453
- To the man in the blue watch cap.

01:13:16.000 --> 01:13:17.114
- [Audience Member] Well
I was just wondering about

01:13:17.114 --> 01:13:19.030
if you could say more about,

01:13:19.030 --> 01:13:20.050
so you talked about New Orleans

01:13:20.050 --> 01:13:22.050
is the American Dream writ large,

01:13:22.050 --> 01:13:23.910
but then you talked about also

01:13:23.910 --> 01:13:26.870
there are protective warnings of dangers

01:13:26.870 --> 01:13:28.560
too threatening to consider.

01:13:28.560 --> 01:13:29.680
And I'm wondering, I don't know,

01:13:29.680 --> 01:13:32.450
just that I was struck
by that, by that idea,

01:13:32.450 --> 01:13:35.233
and I wondered if you
had more to say about it,

01:13:36.220 --> 01:13:38.580
about climate disaster, for example,

01:13:38.580 --> 01:13:42.130
or about the ways in which
the fabric of the union

01:13:43.410 --> 01:13:45.160
feels like it's being rent asunder.

01:13:46.420 --> 01:13:49.060
Yeah, tell me what was
going on in your mind

01:13:49.060 --> 01:13:52.099
when you were writing that,

01:13:52.099 --> 01:13:52.932
what do you have to define that?

01:13:52.932 --> 01:13:55.880
- Okay, I'll try to do
that in as compact a time,

01:13:55.880 --> 01:13:57.160
are we worried about time?

01:13:57.160 --> 01:13:59.103
- No.
- Okay fine, good.

01:14:02.320 --> 01:14:07.320
When I said it was the
American Dream writ large,

01:14:09.170 --> 01:14:13.740
what I mean by that is
that a broad spectrum of

01:14:16.040 --> 01:14:21.040
cultural affiliation is
allowed an extraordinary

01:14:23.830 --> 01:14:28.730
wide pallet upon which
to, each one is able to,

01:14:28.730 --> 01:14:32.490
you have French, Spanish,
you have all of these

01:14:32.490 --> 01:14:35.960
ethnic, regional, national identifiers

01:14:35.960 --> 01:14:40.570
really being out there in
full view, in full expression,

01:14:40.570 --> 01:14:44.480
vigorous full expression,
and yet also at the same time

01:14:44.480 --> 01:14:47.620
contributing to these syncretic forms

01:14:47.620 --> 01:14:50.973
that are combinations of
these different identifiable

01:14:53.070 --> 01:14:56.710
ethnic and whatever else, traditions.

01:14:56.710 --> 01:15:01.710
So to me, that is the
American Dream, for me,

01:15:01.760 --> 01:15:04.720
because I think that the dream,

01:15:04.720 --> 01:15:06.820
which is not necessarily the reality,

01:15:06.820 --> 01:15:10.170
God knows, all the time,
is that we maintain

01:15:14.150 --> 01:15:18.583
a fruitful tension between
where we came from,

01:15:19.770 --> 01:15:21.370
however you might wanna read that,

01:15:21.370 --> 01:15:23.350
and what might be possible for us.

01:15:23.350 --> 01:15:25.380
Whether it's gender transformation,

01:15:25.380 --> 01:15:27.580
whether it's singing music

01:15:27.580 --> 01:15:30.510
from a completely different
tradition than ours,

01:15:30.510 --> 01:15:31.560
as Saul Bellow said,

01:15:31.560 --> 01:15:35.020
whatever you're strong enough
to carry out, it's yours,

01:15:35.020 --> 01:15:36.910
as long as you do something
interesting with it.

01:15:36.910 --> 01:15:40.610
So by American Dream I suppose
that's what I meant by that.

01:15:40.610 --> 01:15:43.800
Now, as far as warnings and dangers, well,

01:15:43.800 --> 01:15:45.043
you said it yourself,

01:15:47.280 --> 01:15:52.280
we also see the American tendency,

01:15:52.430 --> 01:15:53.533
well don't call it an American tendency,

01:15:53.533 --> 01:15:57.680
it's a human tendency, to
figure that we don't need

01:15:57.680 --> 01:16:02.680
to really be super
proactive in our stewardship

01:16:04.330 --> 01:16:09.330
of elements of the geography,
physical or otherwise,

01:16:09.420 --> 01:16:12.480
that are meant to preserve our wellbeing.

01:16:12.480 --> 01:16:16.590
So whether that's, oh, the
levies, yeah right, well,

01:16:16.590 --> 01:16:18.310
I gotta go home and
watch the football game,

01:16:18.310 --> 01:16:21.170
I won't do the inspection
today, or whatever it might be,

01:16:21.170 --> 01:16:23.560
whatever levels of incompetence

01:16:23.560 --> 01:16:27.920
or just bad attention obtained,

01:16:27.920 --> 01:16:29.880
we see that in New Orleans,

01:16:29.880 --> 01:16:32.670
New Orleans has suffered from
that, from climate change

01:16:32.670 --> 01:16:35.760
all the way down to just about everything

01:16:35.760 --> 01:16:38.123
that you could think about about the city.

01:16:39.050 --> 01:16:42.330
And initially, I started to feel

01:16:42.330 --> 01:16:44.410
that the lecture was
getting a little bit long,

01:16:44.410 --> 01:16:47.760
so I cut out a long section that I had

01:16:47.760 --> 01:16:50.750
quoting from Devil Sent the
Rain in a piece that I wrote

01:16:50.750 --> 01:16:53.510
after the BP oil spill, do y'all remember

01:16:53.510 --> 01:16:55.490
the Deep Water Horizon?

01:16:55.490 --> 01:16:58.950
This was 2010, and the pipeline burst,

01:16:58.950 --> 01:17:01.830
and there were millions, or
billions, who's counting,

01:17:01.830 --> 01:17:04.260
gallons of oil being shot out

01:17:04.260 --> 01:17:06.410
into some of the most
ecologically sensitive waters

01:17:06.410 --> 01:17:11.410
on the planet, and everybody kept saying,

01:17:11.960 --> 01:17:15.767
oh you poor guys down there,
first Katrina, now this.

01:17:15.767 --> 01:17:20.580
And I thought, what that was about

01:17:20.580 --> 01:17:25.580
was people not caring enough to make sure

01:17:26.130 --> 01:17:28.900
that there were safety precautions.

01:17:28.900 --> 01:17:31.483
Oh my God, regulation, God forbid,

01:17:33.606 --> 01:17:37.083
and I thought, that's
not you guys down there,

01:17:38.010 --> 01:17:39.580
have you ever heard of ENRON?

01:17:39.580 --> 01:17:40.920
Have you ever heard of WorldCom?

01:17:40.920 --> 01:17:43.540
Sorry, everybody's probably
forgotten about that by now,

01:17:43.540 --> 01:17:45.100
savings and loan scandal?

01:17:45.100 --> 01:17:47.180
Okay, you gotta be of a certain age.

01:17:47.180 --> 01:17:49.720
How about the fact that
the entire world economy

01:17:49.720 --> 01:17:53.360
just about imploded in 2008

01:17:53.360 --> 01:17:58.270
as a result of the loosening
of the intolerable chains

01:17:58.270 --> 01:18:03.171
around the limbs of
the beleaguered banking

01:18:03.171 --> 01:18:06.150
and securities industries?

01:18:06.150 --> 01:18:07.433
I'm sorry, industry.

01:18:08.330 --> 01:18:10.923
Those regulations were so 1933,

01:18:12.572 --> 01:18:14.403
and we got what we were asking for.

01:18:15.739 --> 01:18:19.000
And it's a human problem, but we have seen

01:18:19.000 --> 01:18:24.000
the specific action, I'm
sorry, specific accent,

01:18:24.040 --> 01:18:26.960
in which that problem
has been articulated,

01:18:26.960 --> 01:18:29.080
and it's a very American accent,

01:18:29.080 --> 01:18:33.330
and I would say that
New Orleans has shown us

01:18:33.330 --> 01:18:37.900
some of the most vivid waking dreams

01:18:37.900 --> 01:18:41.064
that could represent that
danger that we've had.

01:18:41.064 --> 01:18:43.330
WorldCom, ENRON, all that stuff

01:18:43.330 --> 01:18:46.100
didn't offer these searing dreams

01:18:46.100 --> 01:18:48.430
to trouble the sleep of the republic,

01:18:48.430 --> 01:18:51.610
and New Orleans has, but
they're representative.

01:18:51.610 --> 01:18:53.366
Yeah, that work for you?

01:18:53.366 --> 01:18:55.370
- Yeah, that works for me.
- Okay.

01:18:55.370 --> 01:18:57.995
Everybody, I wanna thank
you so much, thank you.

01:18:57.995 --> 01:19:01.162
(audience applauding)

01:19:09.133 --> 01:19:12.550
(light, twinkling music)

